items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:28:---
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:29:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:30:## Statement
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:31:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:32:**(a) In ZF.** Every infinite cardinal $\kappa$ ([[def-cardinal]]) equals
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:33:$\aleph_\alpha$ ([[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) for **exactly one** ordinal
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:34:$\alpha$. So the alephs are not merely a supply of infinite cardinals: they are
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:35:all of them, and the operation $\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$ is a bijective
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:36:enumeration of the infinite cardinals by the ordinals.
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:37:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:38:**(b) Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Every infinite
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:39:set is equinumerous ([[def-equinumerous]]) with exactly one aleph.
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:40:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:41:The two clauses say different things, and the difference is the whole content of
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:42:the choice hypothesis. Clause (a) classifies **cardinals**, which are ordinals;
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:43:clause (b) classifies **sets**, and needs to know first that an arbitrary set has
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:44:a cardinality at all.
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:45:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:46:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:47:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:48:**Given:** ZF; the Axiom of Choice only in clause (b).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:49:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:50:[L1] The operation $\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$ is defined at every ordinal, takes infinite cardinal values, is strictly increasing, satisfies $\aleph_0 = \omega$, $\aleph_{\alpha+1} = \aleph_\alpha^{+}$, $\aleph_\lambda = \bigcup\{\aleph_\beta : \beta \in \lambda\}$ at limits, and satisfies $\alpha \le \aleph_\alpha$ ([[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:51:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:52:[L2] $\kappa^{+}$ is the least cardinal strictly above the cardinal $\kappa$ ([[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]]).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:53:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:54:[L3] Every nonempty set of ordinals has an $\in$-least element; ordinals satisfy trichotomy; $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ iff $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$; an ordinal is a transitive set ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[def-ordinal]]).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:55:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:56:[L4] Every ordinal is exactly one of $0$, a successor, or a limit ([[def-limit-ordinal]]); $\omega$ is the least limit ordinal ([[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]]).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:57:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:58:[L5] A cardinal $\kappa$ is finite when $\kappa \in \omega$ and infinite when $\omega \subseteq \kappa$, that is $\omega \le \kappa$ ([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]); and $\omega$ is a cardinal (claim (a) of [[thm-cardinal-arithmetic-agrees-with-finite-counting]], [[def-cardinal]]).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:59:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:60:[L6] For a well-orderable $X$, $\lvert X\rvert$ is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$, $X \approx \lvert X \rvert$, it is a cardinal, and $\lvert \alpha \rvert = \alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:61:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:62:[L7] Assuming the Axiom of Choice, every set carries a well-order ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]], [[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:63:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:64:[L8] A set is finite when it is equinumerous with a natural number ([[def-countable]]).
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:65:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:66:## Proof
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:67:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:68:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:69:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:70:1.1 Let $\kappa$ be an infinite cardinal and put $S = \{\alpha \in \kappa \cup \{\kappa\} : \kappa \le \aleph_\alpha\}$; then $\kappa \in S$, since $\kappa \in \kappa \cup \{\kappa\}$ and $\kappa \le \aleph_\kappa$ by [L1], so $S$ is a nonempty set of ordinals. [L1, L3]
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:71:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:72:1.2 The enumeration is injective: if $\aleph_\alpha = \aleph_\beta$ with $\alpha \ne \beta$ then one of $\alpha \in \beta$, $\beta \in \alpha$ holds by [L3], and strict increase in [L1] makes the two values distinct; so no infinite cardinal is an aleph at two different indices. [L1, L3]
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:73:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:74:2.1 Let $\alpha$ be the $\in$-least element of $S$, which exists by [L3]; then $\kappa \le \aleph_\alpha$, and $\aleph_\beta < \kappa$ for every $\beta \in \alpha$, because $\beta \in \alpha \in \kappa \cup \{\kappa\}$ puts $\beta$ in $\kappa \cup \{\kappa\}$ by transitivity, so $\beta \notin S$ and trichotomy leaves $\aleph_\beta < \kappa$. [step 1.1, L3]
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:75:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:76:3.1 In each of the three cases of [L4] this forces $\kappa = \aleph_\alpha$: if $\alpha = 0$ then $\omega = \aleph_0 \le \kappa \le \aleph_0$ by [L5], so $\kappa = \aleph_0$; if $\alpha = \beta + 1$ then $\aleph_\beta < \kappa$ by step 2.1 and $\kappa$ is a cardinal, so $\aleph_{\beta+1} = \aleph_\beta^{+} \le \kappa$ by [L2], while $\kappa \le \aleph_\alpha$ gives the reverse; and if $\alpha$ is a limit then $\aleph_\beta \subseteq \kappa$ for every $\beta \in \alpha$ by step 2.1, so $\aleph_\alpha = \bigcup\{\aleph_\beta : \beta \in \alpha\} \subseteq \kappa$ by [L1], again with the reverse inequality already in hand. [step 2.1, L1, L2, L3, L4, L5]
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:77:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:78:4.1 Claim (a) is step 3.1 with the uniqueness of step 1.2; and claim (b) follows: assuming the Axiom of Choice an infinite set $X$ is well-orderable by [L7], so $\lvert X \rvert$ exists by [L6] and is not a natural number, since $X \approx \lvert X \rvert$ would then make $X$ finite by [L8], whence $\lvert X \rvert$ is an infinite cardinal by [L5] and $X \approx \lvert X \rvert = \aleph_\alpha$ for exactly one $\alpha$, uniqueness holding because $X \approx \aleph_\alpha$ forces $\aleph_\alpha = \lvert X \rvert$ by [L6]. [step 1.2, step 3.1, L5, L6, L7, L8] ∎
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:79:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:80:## Remarks
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:81:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:82:**What makes the enumeration exhaustive.** Not the recursion, which only produces alephs, but the fact that $\alpha \le \aleph_\alpha$: it guarantees that the alephs eventually overtake any given cardinal, so a least index with $\kappa \le \aleph_\alpha$ exists, and the three-case analysis then shows that "least" forces equality. Without the inequality the search would have no place to start.
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:83:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:84:**Clause (b) is exactly as strong as the well-ordering theorem.** If every infinite set were equinumerous with an aleph then every set would be well-orderable, since an aleph is an ordinal, and that is equivalent to the Axiom of Choice ([[cor-ac-iff-well-ordering]]). So clause (b) is not a theorem of ZF, and it is stated with its hypothesis rather than proved.
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:85:
items/thm-every-infinite-cardinal-is-an-aleph.md:86:**What is enumerated and what is not.** The alephs enumerate the infinite cardinals in increasing order. They do **not** enumerate the values of the power operation: assuming the Axiom of Choice, so that $2^{\aleph_0}$ is a cardinal at all, it is an aleph by clause (a), but which one is not settled by the axioms in use here, and nothing on this page or its companion asserts a value.
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:1:---
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:2:id: thm-cofinality-basics
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:3:kind: theorem
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:4:title: "$\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha) \\le \\alpha$; $\\operatorname{cf}(0) = 0$ and $\\operatorname{cf}(\\alpha + 1) = 1$; for a limit ordinal $\\lambda$ the value $\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$ is an infinite cardinal with $\\operatorname{cf}(\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)) = \\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$, so it is regular; and every cofinal subset of $\\lambda$ has cardinality at least $\\operatorname{cf}(\\lambda)$, a value that is attained"
