items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:28:---
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:29:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:30:## Statement
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:31:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:32:Work in ZF; no choice principle is used. Let $\operatorname{cf}$ be the
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:33:cofinality of [[def-cofinality]]. Then:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:34:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:35:**(a)** $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \le \alpha$ for every ordinal $\alpha$
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:36:([[def-ordinal]]);
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:37:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:38:**(b)** $\operatorname{cf}(0) = 0$, and $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha + 1) = 1$ for
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:39:every ordinal $\alpha$, where $\alpha + 1 = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:40:([[def-ordinal-addition]]);
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:41:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:42:**(c)** for a limit ordinal $\lambda$ ([[def-limit-ordinal]]),
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:43:$\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$ is an **infinite cardinal** ([[def-cardinal]]) and
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:44:$\operatorname{cf}(\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)) = \operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$, so
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:45:$\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$ is a **regular** cardinal;
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:46:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:47:**(d)** for a limit ordinal $\lambda$, every cofinal $C \subseteq \lambda$
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:48:([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]) satisfies
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:49:$\operatorname{cf}(\lambda) \le \lvert C \rvert$, and some cofinal subset of
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:50:$\lambda$ has cardinality exactly $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$.
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:51:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:52:Clause (c) is what discharges the naming obligation of [[def-cofinality]]:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:53:"regular" is defined through $\operatorname{cf}$, and it is a theorem, not a
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:54:convention, that $\operatorname{cf}$ of a limit ordinal is a cardinal at which
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:55:the definition can be tested.
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:56:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:57:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:58:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:59:**Given:** ZF, with no choice principle. Throughout, a map $f : \beta \to \alpha$ is called cofinal when $f[\beta]$ is cofinal in $\alpha$.
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:60:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:61:[L1] $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha)$ is the least ordinal $\beta$ admitting a cofinal $f : \beta \to \alpha$; for that $\beta$ a strictly increasing cofinal $g : \beta \to \alpha$ exists ([[def-cofinality]], [[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:62:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:63:[L2] $C \subseteq \alpha$ is cofinal when every $\zeta \in \alpha$ has some $\eta \in C$ with $\zeta \le \eta$ ([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:64:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:65:[L3] Ordinals: trichotomy; $\alpha \subseteq \beta$ iff $\alpha \in \beta$ or $\alpha = \beta$; $\alpha \notin \alpha$; every element of an ordinal is an ordinal; every set of ordinals is well ordered by $\in$ ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]], [[lem-ordinal-basics]], [[def-well-order]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:66:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:67:[L4] Every ordinal is exactly one of $0$, a successor, or a limit, and $\omega$ is the least limit ordinal ([[def-limit-ordinal]], [[lem-omega-least-limit-ordinal]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:68:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:69:[L5] For a well-orderable $X$: $X \approx \lvert X\rvert$, the value is a cardinal, equinumerous sets receive the same one, and $\lvert \alpha\rvert = \alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]], [[def-cardinal]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:70:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:71:[L6] For cardinals $\kappa \le \lambda$ iff $\kappa \preceq \lambda$, and $A \preceq B$ with both well-orderable gives $\lvert A\rvert \le \lvert B\rvert$ (claim (a) of [[lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:72:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:73:[L7] Every well-order has a unique order type, and the isomorphism onto it is a bijection ([[thm-mostowski-collapse]], [[def-order-isomorphism]], [[def-equinumerous]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:74:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:75:[L8] Precomposing a function with a bijection onto its domain leaves its range unchanged, since $g \circ h$ has image $g[h[\mu]] = g[\beta]$ when $h : \mu \to \beta$ is onto; a strictly increasing map of ordinals is injective, and satisfies $\xi \le \eta \Rightarrow g(\xi) \le g(\eta)$, both by trichotomy ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]], [[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:76:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:77:[L9] A cardinal $\kappa$ is infinite exactly when $\omega \le \kappa$ ([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]).
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:78:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:79:## Proof
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:80:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:81:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:82:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:83:1.1 Claim (a): the identity $\alpha \to \alpha$ is cofinal by [L2], so the least length in [L1] is at most $\alpha$. [L1, L2]
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:84:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:85:1.2 Claim (b): for $\alpha = 0$ the empty map $0 \to 0$ is cofinal vacuously, so $\operatorname{cf}(0) = 0$; for $\alpha + 1$ the map $0 \mapsto \alpha$ is cofinal, since every $\zeta \in \alpha + 1$ satisfies $\zeta \le \alpha$ by [L3], while the empty map into the nonempty $\alpha + 1$ is not, so $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha + 1) = 1$. [L1, L2, L3]
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:86:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:87:1.3 Let $\lambda$ be a limit ordinal, $\beta = \operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$ and $g : \beta \to \lambda$ strictly increasing and cofinal by [L1]; then $\beta$ is a limit ordinal, so $\omega \le \beta$ by [L4]: $\beta \ne 0$ because $0 \in \lambda$ and the empty range is not cofinal, and $\beta = \gamma + 1$ is impossible, since then $g(\eta) \le g(\gamma)$ for all $\eta \in \beta$ by [L8], so cofinality would give $\lambda \subseteq g(\gamma) + 1$ while $g(\gamma) \in \lambda$ gives $g(\gamma) + 1 \subseteq \lambda$, making $\lambda = g(\gamma) + 1$ a successor. [L1, L2, L3, L4, L8]
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:88:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:89:1.4 With $\lambda$, $\beta$, $g$ as above, $\beta$ is a cardinal: if $\mu = \lvert \beta\rvert \in \beta$ then a bijection $h : \mu \to \beta$ makes $g \circ h : \mu \to \lambda$ a map with the same range as $g$, hence cofinal, so the least length would be at most $\mu \in \beta$, contradicting $\beta = \operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$; so $\lvert \beta\rvert = \beta$ and [L5] applies. [L1, L2, L5, L8]
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:90:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:91:2.1 Claim (c): $\beta$ is an infinite cardinal by steps 1.3, 1.4 and [L9]; and writing $\gamma = \operatorname{cf}(\beta)$, step 1.1 gives $\gamma \le \beta$, while a cofinal $k : \gamma \to \beta$ makes $g \circ k : \gamma \to \lambda$ cofinal — given $\zeta \in \lambda$ pick $\xi \in \beta$ with $\zeta \le g(\xi)$, then $\rho \in \gamma$ with $\xi \le k(\rho)$, and $g(\xi) \le g(k(\rho))$ by [L8] — so $\beta = \operatorname{cf}(\lambda) \le \gamma$ and therefore $\operatorname{cf}(\operatorname{cf}(\lambda)) = \operatorname{cf}(\lambda)$. [step 1.1, step 1.3, step 1.4, L1, L2, L8, L9]
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:92:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:93:3.1 Claim (d): a cofinal $C \subseteq \lambda$ is a set of ordinals, well ordered by $\in$ by [L3], with order type $\delta$ and an order isomorphism $e : \delta \to C$ by [L7]; then $e$ is a cofinal map $\delta \to \lambda$, so $\beta \le \delta$ by [L1], and applying [L5] and [L6] gives $\beta = \lvert \beta\rvert \le \lvert \delta\rvert = \lvert C\rvert$ using step 2.1; conversely $g[\beta]$ is cofinal with $\lvert g[\beta]\rvert = \beta$, since $g$ is injective by [L8]. [step 2.1, L1, L2, L3, L5, L6, L7, L8]
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:94:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:95:4.1 Claims (a), (b), (c) and (d) are established, in ZF. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 2.1, step 3.1] ∎
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:96:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:97:## Remarks
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:98:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:99:**Why (c) is restricted to limit ordinals.** At $0$ and at a successor the cofinality is $0$ or $1$, neither of which is an infinite cardinal, and the regular/singular vocabulary is not applied there. Since every infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal, the restriction costs nothing where the notion is used.
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:100:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:101:**What clause (d) is for.** It converts a cofinality question into a counting question: to show $\operatorname{cf}(\lambda) \le \kappa$ it suffices to exhibit **any** cofinal subset of size $\kappa$, with no attention to its order type. That is how every cofinality on the companion page is computed, and the attainment half is what makes the bound sharp.
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:102:
items/thm-cofinality-basics.md:103:**Where the strictly increasing witness is spent.** Three times, and each time essentially: in step 1.3, to know that a witness of successor length would have a largest value; in step 2.1, to know that $g$ preserves $\le$, without which the composite $g \circ k$ need not be cofinal; and in step 3.1, to know that $g$ is injective, without which $g[\beta]$ need not have cardinality $\beta$. That is why [[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]] proves claim (b) rather than stopping at the existence of a least length.

