items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:30:---
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:31:## Statement
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:32:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:33:Let $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$ be topological spaces
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:34:([[def-topological-space]]), and let $\mathbb{R}$ carry its usual topology, the
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:35:metric topology of $d_{\mathbb{R}}(s,t) = |s-t|$
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:36:([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], [[def-metric-topology]],
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:37:[[def-metrizable-space]]). Then:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:38:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:39:1. **Continuous images.** If $f : X \to Y$ is continuous
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:40:   ([[def-continuous-map-top]]) and $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:41:   ([[def-compact-space]]), then $f[X]$ is a compact subset of $Y$. More
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:42:   generally, if $K \subseteq X$ is a compact subset of $X$ then $f[K]$ is a
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:43:   compact subset of $Y$.
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:44:2. **Extreme values.** If $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact and nonempty and
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:45:   $g : X \to \mathbb{R}$ is continuous, then $g[X]$ has a maximum and a minimum
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:46:   ([[def-max-min]]): there are $x_{\max}, x_{\min} \in X$ with
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:47:   $$g(x_{\min}) \;\le\; g(x) \;\le\; g(x_{\max}) \qquad \text{for every } x \in X .$$
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:48:3. **Compact to Hausdorff.** If $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact, $(Y,
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:49:   \mathcal{T}_Y)$ is Hausdorff ([[def-hausdorff-space]]) and $f : X \to Y$ is a
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:50:   continuous bijection, then $f$ is a homeomorphism
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:51:   ([[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:52:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:53:Nonemptiness in claim 2 is a hypothesis and not an oversight: for
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:54:$X = \varnothing$ the image is empty and has neither a maximum nor a minimum. **No
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:55:choice principle is used**: the one selection made below is over a finite index
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:56:set, where [[lem-finite-choice]] is a theorem of ZF.
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:57:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:58:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:59:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:60:**Given:** Topological spaces $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ and $(Y, \mathcal{T}_Y)$, and $\mathbb{R}$ with its usual topology.
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:61:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:62:[L1] A function is continuous exactly when the preimage of every open set is open ([[def-continuous-map-top]], [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], clause (b)).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:63:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:64:[L2] A space is compact exactly when every family of open sets with union the space has a finite subfamily with union the space; a subset $A$ is a compact subset when the subspace $(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:65:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:66:[L3] $A$ is a compact subset of a space $Z$ exactly when for every family $\mathcal{U}$ of open subsets of $Z$ with $A \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$ there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with $A \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n$, or else $A = \varnothing$ ([[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], claim 1).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:67:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:68:[L4] A function with domain a natural number all of whose values are nonempty sets has a choice function, and this is a theorem of ZF ([[lem-finite-choice]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:69:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:70:[L5] For $S \subseteq X$ the restriction $f|_S : S \to Y$ of a continuous $f$ is continuous, since $(f|_S)^{-1}[V] = f^{-1}[V] \cap S$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]], [[def-continuous-map-top]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:71:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:72:[L6] $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ is open in the usual topology exactly when every $x \in U$ admits a real $r > 0$ with $(x-r, x+r) \subseteq U$ ([[lem-real-line-is-a-metric-space]], claim 3; [[def-interval]], [[def-metric-topology]], [[def-metrizable-space]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:73:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:74:[L7] Every set of reals listable as $\{a_0, \dots, a_n\}$ with $n \in \mathbb{N}$ has a maximum and a minimum, each of them one of the listed members ([[lem-finite-set-has-max]], [[def-max-min]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:75:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:76:[L8] The order of [[def-real-order]] makes $\mathbb{R}$ a totally ordered field ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]]), so no real satisfies $s < s$, and $s < t$ together with $t \le s$ is impossible ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:77:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:78:[L9] A closed subset of a compact space is a compact subset of it ([[thm-closed-subspace-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]], claim 1).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:79:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:80:[L10] A compact subset of a Hausdorff space is closed in it ([[thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed]], claim 3; [[def-hausdorff-space]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:81:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:82:[L11] A continuous bijection is a homeomorphism if and only if it is a closed map ([[lem-homeomorphism-criteria]], claim 1; [[def-homeomorphism-and-open-maps]]).
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:83:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:84:## Proof
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:85:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:86:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:87:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:88:1.1 For claim 1 assume $(X, \mathcal{T}_X)$ is compact, let $f : X \to Y$ be continuous, and let $\mathcal{U}$ be a family of open subsets of $Y$ with $f[X] \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$; put $\mathcal{G} := \{\, f^{-1}[U] : U \in \mathcal{U} \,\}$, a family of open subsets of $X$ by [L1], whose union is $X$ because every $x \in X$ has $f(x) \in U$ for some $U \in \mathcal{U}$. [L1, construct]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:89:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:90:2.1 If $X = \varnothing$ then $f[X] = \varnothing$ and the second alternative of [L3] holds; otherwise compactness of $X$ applied to $\mathcal{G}$ gives $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $G_0, \dots, G_n \in \mathcal{G}$ with $X = G_0 \cup \dots \cup G_n$. [L2, step 1.1]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:91:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:92:3.1 For each $j \le n$ the set $S_j := \{\, U \in \mathcal{U} : f^{-1}[U] = G_j \,\}$ is nonempty by the definition of $\mathcal{G}$, and $j \mapsto S_j$ is a function with domain the natural number $\sigma(n)$, so a choice function for its values supplies $U_0, \dots, U_n \in \mathcal{U}$ with $f^{-1}[U_j] = G_j$ for every $j \le n$. [L4, step 2.1]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:93:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:94:4.1 Every point of $f[X]$ is $f(x)$ for some $x \in X$, and $x$ lies in some $G_j = f^{-1}[U_j]$, so $f(x) \in U_j$; hence $f[X] \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n$, and by [L3] the set $f[X]$ is a compact subset of $Y$. [L3, step 2.1, step 3.1]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:95:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:96:5.1 For the second sentence of claim 1 let $K \subseteq X$ be a compact subset, so that the subspace $(K, (\mathcal{T}_X)_K)$ is a compact space by [L2] and $f|_K : K \to Y$ is continuous by [L5]; step 4.1, proved for an arbitrary compact space and an arbitrary continuous map out of it, applies to $f|_K$ and gives that $f|_K[K] = f[K]$ is a compact subset of $Y$. [L2, L5, step 4.1]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:97:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:98:5.2 For claim 2 assume $X$ is compact and nonempty and let $g : X \to \mathbb{R}$ be continuous; by step 4.1 the set $S := g[X]$ is a nonempty compact subset of $\mathbb{R}$. Suppose for the moment that $S$ has no maximum; then every $x \in S$ admits $s \in S$ with $x < s$, so the family $\mathcal{R} := \{\, (-\infty, s) : s \in S \,\}$ covers $S$, and its members are open by [L6], since $x < s$ gives $(x - r, x + r) \subseteq (-\infty, s)$ for $r := s - x > 0$. [L6, L8, step 4.1]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:99:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:100:6.1 By [L3] there are $n \in \mathbb{N}$ and $s_0, \dots, s_n \in S$ with $S \subseteq (-\infty, s_0) \cup \dots \cup (-\infty, s_n)$; by [L7] the set $\{s_0, \dots, s_n\}$ has a maximum, one of the $s_i$ and hence a member of $S$, so it lies in some $(-\infty, s_j)$, giving that maximum $< s_j$ while $s_j \le$ that maximum, which [L8] forbids. So $S$ has a maximum; the same argument with the rays $(s, \infty)$, open by [L6], and the minimum supplied by [L7] shows that $S$ has a minimum. [L3, L6, L7, L8, step 5.2]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:101:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:102:6.2 For claim 3 let $f : X \to Y$ be a continuous bijection with $X$ compact and $Y$ Hausdorff, and let $F \subseteq X$ be closed; then $F$ is a compact subset of $X$ by [L9], so $f[F]$ is a compact subset of $Y$ by step 5.1, and hence closed in $Y$ by [L10]. [L9, L10, step 5.1]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:103:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:104:7.1 The maximum and the minimum of $S$ are members of $S = g[X]$, so there are $x_{\max}, x_{\min} \in X$ with $g(x_{\max})$ the maximum and $g(x_{\min})$ the minimum, and then $g(x_{\min}) \le g(x) \le g(x_{\max})$ for every $x \in X$; this is claim 2. [L7, step 6.1]
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:105:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:106:8.1 Step 6.2 says that $f$ carries closed sets to closed sets, so $f$ is a closed map, and by [L11] a continuous bijection that is closed is a homeomorphism, which is claim 3; claims 1 and 2 were proved at steps 5.1 and 7.1. [L11, step 5.1, step 6.2, step 7.1] ∎
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:107:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:108:## Remarks
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:109:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:110:**Claim 2 is the extreme value theorem, and compactness is the whole of it.** No metric, no completeness argument and no sequence appears: the rays $(-\infty, s)$ with $s \in S$ cover a set with no maximum, and a finite subcover of them is impossible because finitely many reals do have a maximum. The metric statement of the same result is [[thm-extreme-value-metric]], proved there for a compact metric space; by [[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]] it is the present claim applied to a metric topology.
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:111:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:112:**Claim 3 is the reason compactness is worth having when identifying spaces.** Constructing a continuous bijection is usually easy and constructing the inverse explicitly is usually not; claim 3 removes the second task whenever the source is compact and the target is Hausdorff. Both hypotheses are needed: the identity from a set with a finer topology to the same set with a coarser one is a continuous bijection and is not a homeomorphism, and it becomes one under these hypotheses precisely because the finer topology is then compact and the coarser Hausdorff.
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:113:
items/thm-compactness-under-continuous-maps.md:114:**The metric special cases are [[thm-continuous-image-of-a-compact-space-is-compact]] and [[thm-continuous-bijection-from-a-compact-space-has-continuous-inverse]].** Neither is used above; both are the corresponding claim read in a metric topology.
