items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:29:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:30:## Example
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:31:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:32:Write ${}^{\omega}2$ for the set of functions $\omega \to 2 = \{0,1\}$, the
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:33:$2$ here being the von Neumann natural number, and $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$ for
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:34:the power set of $\mathbb{N} = \omega$ ([[def-natural-numbers]]). Then:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:35:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:36:**(a) In ZF, with no choice principle:**
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:37:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:38:$$\mathbb{R} \;\approx\; {}^{\omega}2 \;\approx\; \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$$
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:39:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:40:([[def-equinumerous]], [[def-real-numbers]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:41:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:42:**(b) Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]):
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:43:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:44:$$\lvert \mathbb{R} \rvert \;=\; 2^{\aleph_0} \;=\; \lvert \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \rvert$$
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:45:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:46:([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:47:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:48:The two injections are the classical ones and neither uses a binary expansion.
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:49:One direction is the Cantor set: [[thm-cantor-set-ternary-description]] already
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:50:supplies a bijection from the sequences with values in $\{0,1\}$ onto the Cantor
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:51:set $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ ([[def-cantor-set]]). The other is the cut map
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:52:$x \mapsto \{q \in \mathbb{Q} : q < x\}$, injective because $\mathbb{Q}$ is dense
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:53:in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-of-q-dense]]), and $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{Q})$ is a copy of
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:54:$\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$ because $\mathbb{Q}$ is countable
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:55:([[thm-rationals-countable]]). [[thm-schroder-bernstein]] closes the loop, and it
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:56:is choice free, which is what makes clause (a) a theorem of ZF.
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:57:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:58:## Facts & Assumptions
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:59:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:60:**Given:** $\mathbb{R}$ with its order and the canonical embedding $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{R}$; the Axiom of Choice is assumed only in clause (b).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:61:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:62:[L1] The order of [[def-real-order]] makes $\mathbb{R}$ ([[def-real-numbers]]) a totally ordered field ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]]) with the least-upper-bound property ([[cor-cauchy-reals-lub-complete]]), hence a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) and hence Archimedean ([[thm-of-archimedean]]); and $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to \mathbb{R}$ is the unique order-preserving field embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:63:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:64:[L2] For $x < y$ in an Archimedean ordered field there is a rational $q$ with $x < \iota(q) < y$ ([[lem-of-q-dense]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:65:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:66:[L3] There is a bijection from the set of sequences with values in $\{0,1\}$ onto the Cantor set $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}$ (claim 3 of [[thm-cantor-set-ternary-description]], [[def-cantor-set]]); a sequence of reals is a function $\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$ ([[def-sequence]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:67:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:68:[L4] $\mathbb{Q} \approx \mathbb{N}$ ([[thm-rationals-countable]], [[def-countable]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:69:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:70:[L5] $A \approx B$ implies $\mathcal{P}(A) \approx \mathcal{P}(B)$ and ${}^{C}A \approx {}^{C}B$ ([[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:71:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:72:[L6] If $A \preceq B$ and $B \preceq A$ then $A \approx B$ ([[thm-schroder-bernstein]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:73:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:74:[L7] For a well-orderable set $X$, $\lvert X\rvert$ is the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$ and equinumerous sets receive the same one ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]], [[def-cardinal]]); assuming the Axiom of Choice every set has one ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:75:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:76:[L8] Assuming the Axiom of Choice, $2^{\kappa} = \lvert \mathcal{P}(\kappa)\rvert$, and $\aleph_0 = \omega$ ([[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]], [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:77:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:78:[L9] A subset inclusion is an injection, a composition of injections is an injection, and a map with a two-sided inverse is a bijection ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:79:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:80:## Verification
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:81:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:82:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:83:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:84:1.1 The two-element sets $2 = \{0,1\}$ and $\{0_{\mathbb{R}}, 1_{\mathbb{R}}\}$ are equinumerous, since $0_{\mathbb{R}} \ne 1_{\mathbb{R}}$ in a field, so ${}^{\omega}2 \approx {}^{\omega}\{0_{\mathbb{R}},1_{\mathbb{R}}\}$ by [L5]. [L1, L5, L9]
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:85:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:86:1.2 The set ${}^{\omega}\{0_{\mathbb{R}},1_{\mathbb{R}}\}$ is exactly the set of sequences with values in $\{0,1\}$ of [L3], so it is in bijection with $C \subseteq \mathbb{R}$, and composing with the inclusion gives an injection ${}^{\omega}\{0_{\mathbb{R}},1_{\mathbb{R}}\} \to \mathbb{R}$. [L3, L9]
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:87:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:88:1.3 The map $x \mapsto Q_x := \{\, q \in \mathbb{Q} : \iota(q) < x \,\}$ is an injection $\mathbb{R} \to \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{Q})$: for $x \ne y$ the order is total by [L1], so we may assume $x < y$, and [L2] supplies a rational $q$ with $x < \iota(q) < y$, whence $q \in Q_y$ and $q \notin Q_x$, so $Q_x \ne Q_y$. [L1, L2]
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:89:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:90:1.4 $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{Q}) \approx \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$ by [L4] and [L5]. [L4, L5]
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:91:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:92:1.5 $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N}) \approx {}^{\omega}2$: the map sending $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ to its characteristic function has the two-sided inverse $h \mapsto h^{-1}[\{1\}]$. [L9]
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:93:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:94:2.1 Chaining steps 1.1, 1.2 gives ${}^{\omega}2 \preceq \mathbb{R}$, and chaining steps 1.3, 1.4, 1.5 gives $\mathbb{R} \preceq {}^{\omega}2$; so [L6] yields $\mathbb{R} \approx {}^{\omega}2$, and with step 1.5 also $\mathbb{R} \approx \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$, which is clause (a) and uses no choice principle. [step 1.1, step 1.2, step 1.3, step 1.4, step 1.5, L6, L9]
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:95:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:96:3.1 Assuming the Axiom of Choice, all three sets have cardinalities by [L7], equal by step 2.1, and $\lvert \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})\rvert = 2^{\lvert \omega \rvert} = 2^{\aleph_0}$ by [L8] and [L7]; so $\lvert \mathbb{R}\rvert = 2^{\aleph_0} = \lvert \mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})\rvert$, which is clause (b). [step 2.1, L7, L8] ∎
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:97:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:98:## Remarks
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:99:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:100:**Why binary expansions are avoided.** The textbook proof identifies a real in $[0,1]$ with the set of positions where its binary expansion has a $1$, and then has to deal with the reals having two expansions. Neither injection above meets that difficulty: the Cantor set map is already published as a bijection, and the cut map needs only density. The cost is that the two injections go in opposite directions and [[thm-schroder-bernstein]] is required to combine them, which is free, since that theorem is choice free.
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:101:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:102:**Which half needs the Axiom of Choice, and why.** Clause (a) is an equinumerosity statement and is a theorem of ZF. Clause (b) writes $\lvert \mathbb{R}\rvert$ and $2^{\aleph_0}$ as **cardinals**, that is as ordinals, and in ZF alone $\mathcal{P}(\mathbb{N})$ need not be well-orderable, so those symbols need not denote anything. The hypothesis buys the notation, not the mathematics.
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:103:
items/ex-the-cardinality-of-the-continuum.md:104:**What is still not decided.** Nothing here says which aleph $2^{\aleph_0}$ is. The one constraint proved in this development is that its cofinality is uncountable ([[cor-cofinality-of-a-cardinal-power]]), and the question of whether it is $\aleph_1$ is the continuum hypothesis ([[rem-cardinal-arithmetic-choice-ledger]]).
