items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:30:## Example
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:31:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:32:Let $\mathcal{B}_\ell := \{\, [a,b) : a, b \in \mathbb{R},\ a < b \,\}$
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:33:([[def-interval]]) and let $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ be $\mathbb{R}$ carrying the
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:34:topology for which $\mathcal{B}_\ell$ is a basis
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:35:([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]], [[thm-basis-criterion]]). Then:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:36:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:37:1. $\mathcal{B}_\ell$ **is** a basis for a topology on $\mathbb{R}$.
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:38:2. $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ **is not compact** ([[def-compact-space]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:39:3. $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ **is Lindel&ouml;f** ([[def-compactness-variants]]),
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:40:   assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice ([[def-countable-choice]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:41:4. $\mathbb{R}_\ell \times \mathbb{R}_\ell$ with the product topology
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:42:   ([[def-product-topology]]) **is not Lindel&ouml;f**: the antidiagonal
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:43:   $\Delta := \{\, (x,-x) : x \in \mathbb{R} \,\}$ is an uncountable subset that
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:44:   is closed and carries the discrete topology as a subspace.
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:45:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:46:So Lindel&ouml;fness is not preserved by products, even by the product of a
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:47:space with itself.
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:48:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:49:**This is the same space that appears elsewhere in the library under the name
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:50:Sorgenfrey line**, re-minted here because the published treatment lives on a page
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:51:whose items may not be cited from anywhere; nothing below depends on that
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:52:treatment.
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:53:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:54:## Facts & Assumptions
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:55:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:56:**Given:** $\mathbb{R}$ with its order, the family $\mathcal{B}_\ell$ of half-open intervals $[a,b) = \{t : a \le t < b\}$ with $a<b$, the space $\mathbb{R}_\ell$, and the product $\mathbb{R}_\ell \times \mathbb{R}_\ell$.
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:57:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:58:[L1] A family $\mathcal{B}$ of subsets of a set $X$ is a basis for a unique topology exactly when it covers $X$ and every point of an intersection of two members lies in a member inside that intersection; the topology consists of the sets $U$ such that every $x \in U$ has $B \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B \subseteq U$ ([[thm-basis-criterion]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]], [[def-topological-space]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:59:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:60:[L2] The sets $B \times B'$ with $B, B' \in \mathcal{B}_\ell$ form a basis for the product topology on $\mathbb{R}_\ell \times \mathbb{R}_\ell$, the index set being a natural number ([[def-product-topology]], [[thm-basis-criterion]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:61:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:62:[L3] The order of $\mathbb{R}$ is total ([[def-real-order]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]); for every real $t$ there is $n \in \mathbb{N}$ with $t < \iota(n)$ ([[thm-of-archimedean]], [[def-canonical-natural]]); and for reals $c<d$ there is a rational strictly between them ([[lem-of-q-dense]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:63:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:64:[L4] $\mathbb{Q}$ is countably infinite and $\mathbb{R}$ is uncountable; a set injecting into an at most countable set is at most countable; and the union of two at most countable sets is at most countable, obtained by interleaving two surjections from $\mathbb{N}$ ([[thm-rationals-countable]], [[thm-r-uncountable]], [[def-countable]], [[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:65:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:66:[L5] A space is Lindel&ouml;f when every open cover has an at most countable subcover, and compact when every open cover has a finite subcover ([[def-compactness-variants]], [[def-compact-space]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:67:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:68:[L6] Countable choice: for every family $(Y_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets there is $f$ on $\mathbb{N}$ with $f(n) \in Y_n$ ([[def-countable-choice]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:69:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:70:[L7] The open sets of a subspace are the traces of the ambient open sets, and its closed sets the traces of the ambient closed sets ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]).
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:71:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:72:## Verification
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:73:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:74:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:75:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:76:1.1 Claim 1: $\mathcal{B}_\ell$ covers $\mathbb{R}$, since $x \in [x, x+1)$; and $[a,b) \cap [c,d)$ is $[\max\{a,c\}, \min\{b,d\})$ when that is nonempty and $\varnothing$ otherwise, so it is a member of $\mathcal{B}_\ell$ or empty. By [L1] the family is a basis for exactly one topology, and a set is open in $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ exactly when each of its points has a half-open interval around it inside it. [L1, L3]
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:77:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:78:2.1 Claim 2: the family $\{\, [-\iota(n), \iota(n)) : n \in \mathbb{N},\ n \ge 1 \,\}$ consists of members of $\mathcal{B}_\ell$, hence of open sets, and covers $\mathbb{R}$ by [L3]; the members increase with $n$, so a finite subfamily has union $[-\iota(N), \iota(N))$ for the largest index $N$ occurring, which omits $\iota(N)$. So $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ is not compact. [L3, L5, step 1.1]
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:79:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:80:2.2 For claim 3 let $\mathcal{A}$ be an open cover of $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ and let $\mathcal{D}$ be the family of members of $\mathcal{B}_\ell$ contained in some member of $\mathcal{A}$; by step 1.1 the family $\mathcal{D}$ covers $\mathbb{R}$. Put $C := \bigcup \{\, (a,b) : [a,b) \in \mathcal{D} \,\}$. [L5, step 1.1, construct]
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:81:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:82:2.3 In $\mathbb{R}_\ell \times \mathbb{R}_\ell$ the antidiagonal $\Delta$ is discrete as a subspace: for $x \in \mathbb{R}$ the basic set $[x, x+1) \times [-x, -x+1)$ meets $\Delta$ only in $(x,-x)$, since a point $(y,-y)$ in it satisfies $x \le y$ and $-x \le -y$, that is $y \le x$. By [L7] each singleton of $\Delta$ is therefore open in the subspace. [L2, L3, L7, step 1.1]
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:83:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:84:2.4 $\Delta$ is closed in $\mathbb{R}_\ell \times \mathbb{R}_\ell$: let $(u,v)$ have $u + v \ne 0$. If $u + v > 0$, every point $(y_1,y_2)$ of $[u,u+1) \times [v,v+1)$ has $y_1 + y_2 \ge u+v > 0$, so the box misses $\Delta$. If $u+v < 0$, put $\delta := -(u+v)/2 > 0$; every point of $[u, u+\delta) \times [v, v+\delta)$ has $y_1 + y_2$ at least $u+v$ and less than $u+v+2\delta = 0$, so again the box misses $\Delta$. So the complement of $\Delta$ is open. [L2, L3, step 1.1]
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:85:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:86:3.1 $\mathbb{R} \setminus C$ is at most countable. Fix a surjection $\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{Q}$ ([L4]) and for $x \notin C$ let $r(x)$ be the rational of least index with $x < r(x)$ and $[x, r(x)) \in \mathcal{D}$; such rationals exist, since $\mathcal{D}$ covers gives $[a,b) \in \mathcal{D}$ with $a \le x < b$, a rational $q$ with $x < q < b$ by [L3] then has $[x,q) \subseteq [a,b)$ and so $[x,q) \in \mathcal{D}$. Nothing is selected, the least index being determined by $x$. The map $r$ is injective on $\mathbb{R} \setminus C$: if $x < y$ lay outside $C$ with $r(x) = r(y) = q$, then $[x,q) \in \mathcal{D}$ gives $(x,q) \subseteq C$ and $x < y < q$ puts $y$ in $C$. So $\mathbb{R} \setminus C$ injects into $\mathbb{Q}$ and is at most countable by [L4]. [L3, L4, step 2.2]
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:87:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:88:3.2 $C$ is covered by the at most countable family $\mathcal{D}_{\mathbb{Q}} := \{\, [p,q) \in \mathcal{D} : p, q \in \mathbb{Q} \,\}$, at most countable by [L4] as a subfamily of a set indexed by pairs of rationals: given $x \in C$ there is $[a,b) \in \mathcal{D}$ with $a < x < b$, and [L3] gives rationals $p, q$ with $a < p < x < q < b$, whence $[p,q) \subseteq [a,b)$ lies in $\mathcal{D}$ and contains $x$. [L3, L4, step 2.2]
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:89:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:90:4.1 So $\mathcal{D}_0 := \mathcal{D}_{\mathbb{Q}} \cup \{\, [x, r(x)) : x \in \mathbb{R} \setminus C \,\}$ is an at most countable subfamily of $\mathcal{D}$ by [L4] and covers $\mathbb{R}$ by steps 3.1 and 3.2. Every member of $\mathcal{D}$ lies inside some member of $\mathcal{A}$, so [L6] applied to an indexing of $\mathcal{D}_0$ by $\mathbb{N}$ supplies one member of $\mathcal{A}$ for each member of $\mathcal{D}_0$, and those form an at most countable subcover of $\mathcal{A}$. Hence $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ is Lindel&ouml;f: claim 3. [L4, L5, L6, step 3.1, step 3.2]
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:91:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:92:5.1 $\Delta$ is uncountable, being in bijection with $\mathbb{R}$ under $x \mapsto (x,-x)$ and $\mathbb{R}$ being uncountable by [L4]. Were $\mathbb{R}_\ell \times \mathbb{R}_\ell$ Lindel&ouml;f, its closed subspace $\Delta$ would be too: given a cover of $\Delta$ by traces of ambient open sets, adjoining the complement of $\Delta$ gives an ambient open cover, an at most countable subcover of it traces back to an at most countable subcover of $\Delta$. But $\Delta$ is discrete by step 2.3, so its singletons form an open cover admitting only itself as a subcover, and that family is uncountable. So $\mathbb{R}_\ell \times \mathbb{R}_\ell$ is not Lindel&ouml;f: claim 4. [L4, L5, L7, step 2.3, step 2.4] ∎
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:93:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:94:## Remarks
