items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:99:  re-minted here rather than cited. Nothing above depends on the published
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:100:  treatment.
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:101:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:102:**What fails in the product.** Lindel&ouml;fness of $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ rests on the rationals being dense and at most countable, so that a cover can be thinned to countably many rational-endpoint intervals plus countably many exceptional points. In the square each point $(x,-x)$ of the antidiagonal has a basic box around it meeting the antidiagonal in that point alone, and there are uncountably many such points; no countability of the rationals helps, because those boxes are pairwise distinct and each of them isolates one antidiagonal point, so an at most countable subfamily of the cover they generate can reach only at most countably many of them.
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:103:
items/ex-the-sorgenfrey-line-is-lindelof-and-its-square-is-not.md:104:**Neither compactness nor Lindel&ouml;fness is what separates the topologies.** $\mathbb{R}_\ell$ is finer than the usual topology of $\mathbb{R}$, since every $(a,b)$ is a union of half-open intervals, and both spaces are Lindel&ouml;f and not compact; the difference shows up only in the square.

