items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:30:---
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:31:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:32:## Statement
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:33:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:34:Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals converging to $x \in \mathbb{R}$
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:35:([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]). Then $(x_k)$ is Cauchy
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:36:([[def-real-limit]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:37:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:38:## Facts & Assumptions
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:39:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:40:**Given:** A sequence $(x_k)$ of reals converging to a real $x$ ([[def-sequence]], [[def-real-limit]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:41:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:42:[L1] $(x_k)$ converges to $x$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x| < \hat\varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$; and $(x_k)$ is Cauchy when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x_l| < \hat\varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge K$ ([[def-real-limit]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:43:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:44:[L2] Triangle inequality: $|a + b| \le |a| + |b|$ in $\mathbb{R}$ ([[lem-of-triangle-inequality]], [[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:45:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:46:[L3] Absolute value: $|-u| = |u|$ for every real $u$ ([[lem-of-abs-value]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:47:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:48:[L4] Halving a positive rational: if $\varepsilon$ is a rational $> 0$ then $\varepsilon/2$ is again a rational, it is $> 0$, and $\varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon$. In detail, $\mathbb{Q}$ is an ordered field ([[thm-rat-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]], [[def-field]]), so $1 > 0$ ([[cor-of-one-positive]]) and $2 := 1 + 1 > 0$ because the positives are closed under addition ([[def-ordered-field]]); hence $2 \ne 0$ is invertible with $2^{-1} > 0$ ([[lem-of-inverse-positive]]), the product $\varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1}$ of two positives is positive ([[lem-of-sign-rules]]), and $\varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1} + \varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1} = \varepsilon \cdot 2^{-1} \cdot 2 = \varepsilon$ by the field axioms ([[def-field]]). The embedding of $\mathbb{Q}$ in $\mathbb{R}$ preserves the order ([[lem-rat-embeds-dense]]), so these facts hold verbatim for the images, under the identification recorded in [[def-sequence]].
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:49:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:50:[L5] The order on $\mathbb{N}$ is total and transitive, so a single threshold serves for both indices ([[thm-nat-linear-order]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:51:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:52:[L6] Order arithmetic in $\mathbb{R}$: adding two strict inequalities, $a < b$ and $c < d$ give $a + c < b + d$ ([[lem-of-add-order]]); and, since $u \le v$ means $u < v$ or $u = v$, the mixed form $u \le v < w \Rightarrow u < w$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]], [[def-ordered-field]]).
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:53:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:54:## Proof
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:55:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:56:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:57:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:58:1.1 Let $\varepsilon > 0$ be rational; then $\varepsilon/2$ is a rational $> 0$. [L4]
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:59:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:60:2.1 By convergence there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon/2$ for all $k \ge K$. [step 1.1, L1]
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:61:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:62:3.1 For all $k, l \ge K$ we get $|x_k - x_l| = |(x_k - x) + (x - x_l)| \le |x_k - x| + |x - x_l| = |x_k - x| + |x_l - x|$, while adding the two strict inequalities of step 2.1 gives $|x_k - x| + |x_l - x| < \varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2 = \varepsilon$; composing the non-strict inequality with the strict one yields $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$. [step 2.1, L2, L3, L4, L5, L6]
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:63:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:64:4.1 Since the rational $\varepsilon > 0$ was arbitrary and the single threshold $K$ works for both indices, $(x_k)$ is Cauchy. [step 3.1, L1] ∎
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:65:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:66:## Remarks
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:67:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:68:- The converse is a genuine theorem and is **not** proved here. "Every Cauchy sequence of reals converges" is the completeness of $\mathbb{R}$ in the Cauchy sense. It is the subject of the next page of this track, *Monotone Sequences, Bolzano-Weierstrass, and Cauchy Completeness*, where it is proved from the least-upper-bound property, last of the four completeness results there, by way of Bolzano-Weierstrass, which is itself routed through the monotone convergence theorem. That proof is not available at this point in the reading order; the converse itself, for the $\mathbb{R}$ this library constructs, already is, by the different route the next remark records.
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:69:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:70:- The converse is nevertheless already available in this library, and it should be said plainly rather than left to the next page. The $\mathbb{R}$ used throughout ([[def-real-numbers]], [[def-sequence]]) is the quotient of the ring of Cauchy sequences of rationals, and [[thm-reals-cauchy-complete]] proves for precisely that $\mathbb{R}$ that every Cauchy sequence of reals converges to a real. Nothing further is needed to have the converse in hand here; and any other complete ordered field inherits it, since any two are isomorphic by a unique ordered-field isomorphism ([[thm-uniqueness-complete-ordered-field]]). The reason the next page proves it again, from the least-upper-bound property, is that that proof is the form the rest of analysis uses and does not route through a particular construction.
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:71:
items/lem-convergent-implies-cauchy.md:72:- The splitting $\varepsilon/2 + \varepsilon/2$ is the whole content. It is worth noticing that no property of $\mathbb{R}$ beyond the ordered-field axioms and the triangle inequality is used, so the same argument shows that a convergent sequence of rationals is Cauchy in $\mathbb{Q}$.
