items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:29:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:30:## Statement
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:31:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:32:Let $R = \omega_1 \times [0,1)$ be the closed long ray with its lexicographic
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:33:order and its order topology ([[def-the-long-line]]), with least element $0_R$
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:34:and no greatest element. For $y \in R$ write
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:35:$[0_R, y] := \{\, z \in R : z \le y \,\}$. Then:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:36:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:37:1. **Initial segments are compact.** For every $u \in R$ the set $[0_R, u]$ is a
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:38:   compact subset of $R$ ([[def-compact-space]]).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:39:2. **Countable compactness, assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice**
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:40:   ([[def-countable-choice]]): $R$ is countably compact
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:41:   ([[def-compactness-variants]]).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:42:3. **$R$ is not compact**, and this needs no choice principle.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:43:4. **$R$ is not Lindel&ouml;f, assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice.**
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:44:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:45:Claims 1 and 3 are theorems of ZF. Claims 2 and 4 spend countable choice, in
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:46:both cases only through claim 3 of
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:47:[[thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum]], which carries the
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:48:hypothesis in its own statement; claim 2 spends it once more to pick a point in
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:49:each of countably many nonempty sets.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:50:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:51:## Facts & Assumptions
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:52:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:53:**Given:** The closed long ray $R$ with its lexicographic order and order topology, its least element $0_R$, the open rays $R_{<b} = \{z : z < b\}$ and $R_{>a} = \{z : a < z\}$, and the open intervals $(a,b)$.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:54:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:55:[A1] The Axiom of Countable Choice, for claims 2 and 4 only ([[def-countable-choice]]).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:56:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:57:[L1] $R$ is a linearly ordered set with least element $0_R$ and no greatest element, carrying the order topology; $R$ itself, the open rays and the open intervals form a basis for that topology, so every open $U$ and every $x \in U$ admit one of them between them ([[def-the-long-line]], [[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]], [[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:58:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:59:[L2] $R$ is a linear continuum: it is order-dense, so between any two of its elements lies a third, and every nonempty subset bounded above has a least upper bound ([[thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum]], claim 1; [[def-upper-bound]]).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:60:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:61:[L3] Assuming $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, every at most countable subset of $R$ has an upper bound in $R$ ([[thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum]], claim 3; [[def-countable]]).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:62:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:63:[L4] A space is compact when every open cover has a finite subcover, countably compact when every at most countable open cover has a finite subcover, and Lindel&ouml;f when every open cover has an at most countable subcover ([[def-compact-space]], [[def-compactness-variants]], [[def-topological-space]]).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:64:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:65:[L5] $A$ is a compact subset of $R$ exactly when every family of open subsets of $R$ covering $A$ has finitely many members covering $A$, or else $A = \varnothing$ ([[lem-compactness-of-a-subspace-is-ambient]], claim 1).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:66:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:67:[L6] For a nonempty set, being at most countable and admitting a surjection from $\mathbb{N}$ are the same thing: a nonempty at most countable family may be listed as $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ with repetitions allowed, and conversely the range of any such list is at most countable; no choice principle is involved ([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]], [[def-countable]]).
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:68:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:69:## Proof
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:70:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:71:**Proof technique:** direct.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:72:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:73:1.1 For claim 1 fix $u \in R$ and a family $\mathcal{U}$ of open subsets of $R$ with $[0_R,u] \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{U}$, and put $D := \{\, y \in [0_R,u] : \text{finitely many members of } \mathcal{U} \text{ cover } [0_R,y] \,\}$. Then $0_R \in D$, since $0_R$ lies in some member of $\mathcal{U}$ and $[0_R, 0_R] = \{0_R\}$, and $u$ is an upper bound of $D$; so [L2] gives $s := \sup D$, and $s \le u$ because $u$ is an upper bound. [L1, L2, L5, construct]
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:74:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:75:1.2 For claim 3 the family $\mathcal{R} := \{\, R_{<x} : x \in R \,\}$ is an open cover of $R$: its members are open by [L1], and every $y \in R$ lies in $R_{<x}$ for some $x$, since $R$ has no greatest element. [L1]
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:76:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:77:1.3 For claim 2 assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ and let $\mathcal{U}$ be an at most countable open cover of $R$ with no finite subcover; it is nonempty, so [L6] lists it as $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$, and $E_n := R \setminus (U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_n)$ is nonempty for every $n$, so countable choice supplies a sequence $(x_n)$ with $x_n \in E_n$. The range $\{\, x_n : n \in \mathbb{N} \,\}$ is at most countable by [L6], so [L3] gives an upper bound $u \in R$ for it. [A1, L3, L4, L6]
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:78:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:79:2.1 $s$ lies in some $U \in \mathcal{U}$, and [L1] gives a basic $B$ with $s \in B \subseteq U$, where $B$ is $R$, an open ray or an open interval. If $B$ is $R$ or a lower ray $R_{<b}$, then every $z \le s$ satisfies $z < b$, so $[0_R,s] \subseteq B \subseteq U$ and $\{U\}$ covers $[0_R,s]$. Otherwise $B$ is $R_{>a}$ or $(a,b)$ with $a < s$, and $a$ is not an upper bound of $D$, so some $y \in D$ has $a < y \le s$; a finite $\mathcal{F} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$ covers $[0_R,y]$, and $[0_R,s] \subseteq [0_R,y] \cup (a,s] \subseteq \bigcup \mathcal{F} \cup B$, so $\mathcal{F} \cup \{U\}$ covers $[0_R,s]$. In every case $s \in D$. [L1, L2, step 1.1]
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:80:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:81:2.2 No finite subfamily of $\mathcal{R}$ covers $R$: the empty subfamily covers $\varnothing$ and $R$ is nonempty, while a subfamily $R_{<x_0}, \dots, R_{<x_n}$ has union $R_{<x}$ for $x$ the greatest of the $x_j$, which exists because the order is linear and the list finite, and $x \notin R_{<x}$. So $R$ is not compact, which is claim 3. [L1, L4, step 1.2]
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:82:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:83:3.1 $s = u$. Suppose $s < u$, and keep $B$ and $U$ from step 2.1, together with a finite $\mathcal{G} \subseteq \mathcal{U}$ covering $[0_R,s]$, which step 2.1 provides. If $B$ is $R$ or $R_{>a}$, then $(s,u] \subseteq B \subseteq U$ and $\mathcal{G} \cup \{U\}$ covers $[0_R,u]$, putting $u$ in $D$ and forcing $u \le s$, contrary to $s < u$. If $B$ is $R_{<b}$ or $(a,b)$, then $s < b$ and $s < u$, so the lesser of $b$ and $u$ is strictly above $s$ and [L2] gives $z$ with $s < z$ and $z$ below that lesser element; then $z \le u$ and $(s,z] \subseteq B$, so $\mathcal{G} \cup \{U\}$ covers $[0_R,z]$ and $z \in D$ with $z > s$, contradicting $s = \sup D$. [L1, L2, step 1.1, step 2.1]
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:84:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:85:4.1 By steps 2.1 and 3.1 the element $u$ lies in $D$, so finitely many members of $\mathcal{U}$ cover $[0_R,u]$; as $\mathcal{U}$ was arbitrary, [L5] makes $[0_R,u]$ a compact subset of $R$, which is claim 1. [L5, step 1.1, step 2.1, step 3.1]
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:86:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:87:5.1 By claim 1 the set $[0_R,u]$ is covered by finitely many of the $U_n$, say by $U_{n_0}, \dots, U_{n_p}$; let $N$ be the greatest of $n_0, \dots, n_p$. Then $x_N \le u$, so $x_N \in [0_R,u] \subseteq U_{n_0} \cup \dots \cup U_{n_p} \subseteq U_0 \cup \dots \cup U_N$, contradicting $x_N \in E_N$. So no such $\mathcal{U}$ exists and $R$ is countably compact, which is claim 2. [L4, L5, step 1.3, step 4.1]
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:88:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:89:6.1 For claim 4 assume $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$ and let $\mathcal{V} \subseteq \mathcal{R}$ be an at most countable subfamily of the cover of step 1.2. The map $x \mapsto R_{<x}$ is injective, since $x < x'$ puts $x$ in $R_{<x'}$ and not in $R_{<x}$, so $A := \{\, x \in R : R_{<x} \in \mathcal{V} \,\}$ is at most countable and [L3] gives it an upper bound $w \in R$; then $R_{<x} \subseteq R_{<w}$ for every $x \in A$, so $\bigcup \mathcal{V} \subseteq R_{<w}$ and $w$ is covered by no member of $\mathcal{V}$. So $\mathcal{R}$ has no at most countable subcover and $R$ is not Lindel&ouml;f, which is claim 4; with claims 1, 2 and 3 at steps 4.1, 5.1 and 2.2 the theorem is proved. [A1, L3, L4, step 1.2, step 2.2, step 5.1] ∎
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:90:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:91:## Remarks
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:92:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:93:**The long ray is the standard example of a countably compact space that is not compact.** Both halves come from the same feature: an at most countable subset of $R$ is bounded above, so countably many open sets can never exhaust it unless finitely many of them already do, while the uncountable cover by initial rays climbs forever. The ordinal $\omega_1$ behaves the same way ([[thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness]]) and for the same reason, which is why both are proved from a boundedness theorem rather than from any covering argument.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:94:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:95:**Claim 1 is a Heine-Borel theorem for a linear continuum.** Its proof uses only that $R$ is order-dense with the least upper bound property, together with the description of the order topology by rays and intervals; no metric and no countability appears. The same argument proves that a closed bounded interval of $\mathbb{R}$ is compact, which is why the two look alike.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:96:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact.md:97:**What is not claimed.** Nothing above says $R$ is sequentially compact, and nothing says it is metrizable or first countable; a countably compact space need not be sequentially compact without further hypotheses, and the implications that do hold are collected in [[thm-compactness-variants-hierarchy]].

