items/def-cardinal.md:41:## Definition
items/def-cardinal.md:42:
items/def-cardinal.md:43:Write $X \approx Y$, and say $X$ and $Y$ are **equinumerous**, when there is a
items/def-cardinal.md:44:bijection $X \to Y$.
items/def-cardinal.md:45:
items/def-cardinal.md:46:An ordinal $\kappa$ ([[def-ordinal]]) is a **cardinal**, equivalently an
items/def-cardinal.md:47:**initial ordinal**, when
items/def-cardinal.md:48:
items/def-cardinal.md:49:$$\text{no } \alpha \in \kappa \text{ satisfies } \alpha \approx \kappa,$$
items/def-cardinal.md:50:
items/def-cardinal.md:51:that is, $\kappa$ is not equinumerous with any strictly smaller ordinal.
items/def-cardinal.md:52:
items/def-cardinal.md:53:**Cardinality, under the Axiom of Choice.** Assume the Axiom of Choice
items/def-cardinal.md:54:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) and let $X$ be a set. Then $X$ carries a well-order
items/def-cardinal.md:55:([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]), which has an order type $\alpha$
items/def-cardinal.md:56:([[thm-mostowski-collapse]]) and in particular $\alpha \approx X$. Now
items/def-cardinal.md:57:$\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ is an ordinal ([[lem-ordinal-basics]],
items/def-cardinal.md:58:claim (c)) whose elements are ordinals (claim (a)) and which contains $\alpha$,
items/def-cardinal.md:59:so $C = \{\xi \in \alpha^{+} : \xi \approx X\}$ is a nonempty set of ordinals and
items/def-cardinal.md:60:has an $\in$-least element $\kappa$ ([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]). This $\kappa$ is the
items/def-cardinal.md:61:**cardinality** of $X$, written $|X|$; it is a cardinal, because
items/def-cardinal.md:62:$\beta \in \kappa$ with $\beta \approx \kappa \approx X$ would lie in
items/def-cardinal.md:63:$\alpha^{+}$ and contradict the minimality of $\kappa$.
items/def-cardinal.md:64:
items/def-cardinal.md:65:**Well-definedness: $\kappa$ does not depend on the well-order or on $\alpha$.**
items/def-cardinal.md:66:The recipe above instantiates a well-order of $X$ and an order type $\alpha$ for
items/def-cardinal.md:67:it, and $X$ will in general carry many well-orders with many different order
items/def-cardinal.md:68:types, so the value $\kappa$ has to be shown independent of both. It is, because
items/def-cardinal.md:69:$\kappa$ is in fact the least ordinal equinumerous with $X$ **outright**, a
items/def-cardinal.md:70:description in which neither the well-order nor $\alpha$ appears. Let $\beta$ be
items/def-cardinal.md:71:any ordinal with $\beta \approx X$. By trichotomy for ordinals
items/def-cardinal.md:72:([[lem-ordinal-trichotomy]]) exactly one of $\beta \in \alpha^{+}$,
items/def-cardinal.md:73:$\beta = \alpha^{+}$, $\alpha^{+} \in \beta$ holds. In the first case
items/def-cardinal.md:74:$\beta \in C$, so $\kappa \subseteq \beta$ by minimality of $\kappa$. In the
items/def-cardinal.md:75:other two cases claim (f) of [[lem-ordinal-basics]] gives
items/def-cardinal.md:76:$\alpha^{+} \subseteq \beta$, and $\alpha \in \alpha^{+}$ because
items/def-cardinal.md:77:$\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$, so $\alpha \in \beta$ and hence
items/def-cardinal.md:78:$\alpha \subseteq \beta$ by claim (f) again; and $\alpha \in C$, so
items/def-cardinal.md:79:$\kappa \subseteq \alpha$ by minimality, whence $\kappa \subseteq \beta$. In every case
items/def-cardinal.md:80:$\kappa \subseteq \beta$, that is $\kappa \le \beta$. So $\kappa$ is the least
items/def-cardinal.md:81:element of the collection of **all** ordinals equinumerous with $X$, and any two
items/def-cardinal.md:82:runs of the recipe, from any two well-orders of $X$, return the same $\kappa$.
items/def-cardinal.md:83:
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:33:## Definition
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:34:
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:35:Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]).
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:36:
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:37:- $\alpha$ is a **successor ordinal** when $\alpha = \beta^{+} = \beta \cup \{\beta\}$
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:38:  for some ordinal $\beta$, which is then an ordinal by [[lem-ordinal-basics]];
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:39:- $\alpha$ is a **limit ordinal** when $\alpha \ne 0$ and $\alpha$ is not a
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:40:  successor ordinal.
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:41:
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:42:Every ordinal is therefore exactly one of: $0$, a successor ordinal, or a limit
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:43:ordinal. The three cases are the three clauses of a definition or a proof by
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:44:transfinite recursion or induction over the ordinals.
items/def-limit-ordinal.md:45:
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:40:## Statement
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:41:
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:42:Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). Say that a function
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:43:$f : \beta \to \alpha$ is **cofinal** when its range
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:44:$f[\beta] = \{f(\xi) : \xi \in \beta\}$ is a cofinal subset of $\alpha$
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:45:([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]), that is, when for every $\zeta \in \alpha$
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:46:there is $\xi \in \beta$ with $\zeta \le f(\xi)$. Then, in ZF:
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:47:
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:48:**(a)** there is a **least** ordinal $\beta$ for which some cofinal
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:49:$f : \beta \to \alpha$ exists;
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:50:
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:51:**(b)** for that least $\beta$ a cofinal $g : \beta \to \alpha$ can be taken
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:52:**strictly increasing**: $\eta \in \xi \in \beta$ implies $g(\eta) \in g(\xi)$.
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:53:
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:54:**No choice principle is used.** The least ordinal of claim (a) is a least
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:55:element of a set of ordinals, and the map of claim (b) is built by transfinite
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:56:recursion from a formula.
items/lem-cofinality-is-well-defined.md:57:
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:37:## Definition
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:38:
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:39:Let $A$ and $B$ be sets and let $f : A \to B$ be a function.
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:40:
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:41:- $f$ is **injective** (one-to-one) if $f(x) = f(y)$ implies $x = y$, for all
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:42:  $x, y \in A$.
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:43:- $f$ is **surjective** (onto) if for every $b \in B$ there is some $x \in A$
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:44:  with $f(x) = b$; equivalently, the image $f[A] := \{ f(x) : x \in A \}$ equals
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:45:  $B$.
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:46:- $f$ is **bijective** if it is both injective and surjective.
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:47:
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:48:For $S \subseteq A$ we write $f[S] = \{ f(x) : x \in S \}$ for the image of $S$,
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:49:and for $T \subseteq B$ we write $f^{-1}[T] = \{ x \in A : f(x) \in T \}$ for the
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:50:preimage of $T$.
items/def-injection-surjection-bijection.md:51:
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:40:## Statement
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:41:
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:42:For ordinals $\alpha$ and $\beta$ ([[def-ordinal]]), **exactly one** of
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:43:
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:44:$$\alpha \in \beta, \qquad \alpha = \beta, \qquad \beta \in \alpha$$
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:45:
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:46:holds. Moreover every nonempty **set** $A$ of ordinals has an $\in$-least
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:47:element, and consequently $A$ ordered by
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:48:$\alpha \le \beta :\iff \alpha \subseteq \beta$ is a well-order
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:49:([[def-well-order]]) whose strict part is membership.
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:50:
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:51:So the ordinals are linearly ordered by $\in$, every set of them is well
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:52:ordered, and [[thm-transfinite-induction]] is available on any such set. No
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:53:choice principle is used.
items/lem-ordinal-trichotomy.md:54:

