items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:29:## Definition
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:30:
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:31:Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]) and $S \subseteq F$.
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:32:
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:33:- $u \in F$ is an **upper bound** of $S$ if $s \le u$ for all $s \in S$; $S$ is
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:34:  **bounded above** if it has an upper bound.
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:35:- $u \in F$ is a **least upper bound** (or **supremum**, $\sup S$) of $S$ if $u$
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:36:  is an upper bound of $S$ and $u \le u'$ for every upper bound $u'$ of $S$.
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:37:
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:38:$F$ is a **complete ordered field** (equivalently, $F$ has the
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:39:**least-upper-bound property**, or is **Dedekind complete**) if every nonempty
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:40:$S \subseteq F$ that is bounded above has a least upper bound in $F$.
items/def-complete-ordered-field.md:41:
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:34:## Statement
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:35:
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:36:Every complete ordered field $F$ ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]) is Archimedean:
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:37:for every $x \in F$ there is a natural number $n \ge 1$ with $x < n \cdot 1_F$,
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:38:where $n \cdot 1_F$ is the canonical natural of the ordered field $F$
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:39:([[def-ordered-field]]). Equivalently, the canonical naturals are cofinal in $F$.
items/thm-of-archimedean.md:40:
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:34:## Statement
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:35:
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:36:Let $F$ be an ordered field ([[def-ordered-field]]). There is a unique field
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:37:homomorphism $\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ ([[def-field-homomorphism]]). On the
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:38:integers it is given by $n \mapsto n \cdot 1_F$ (with $-n \mapsto -(n \cdot 1_F)$
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:39:and $0 \mapsto 0$), and on a rational written as $p/q$ with $q \ge 1$ by
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:40:$\iota(p/q) = \iota(p)\,(q \cdot 1_F)^{-1}$. Moreover $\iota$ is injective and
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:41:order-preserving, so it is an embedding of $\mathbb{Q}$ as an ordered subfield of
items/lem-of-q-embeds.md:42:$F$, and it is the only field homomorphism $\mathbb{Q} \to F$.
