items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:42:## Statement
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:43:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:44:Let $A$ be a nonempty set. Then $A$ is at most countable ([[def-countable]]) if
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:45:and only if there is a surjection $s : \mathbb{N} \to A$
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:46:([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:47:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:48:Moreover, from any such surjection an injection $A \to \mathbb{N}$ is obtained
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:49:explicitly, without any choice, by
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:50:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:51:$$j(a) := \min\{\, k \in \mathbb{N} : s(k) = a \,\}.$$
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:52:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:53:This is the working form of countability used everywhere below: to prove a
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:54:nonempty set countable it suffices to list its elements, repetitions and all.
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:55:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:56:**No choice principle is used.** The backward direction is where an appeal to
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:57:choice would be natural ("for each $a$ pick some $k$ with $s(k) = a$") and it is
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:58:avoided outright, because $\min$ is canonical: every nonempty set of naturals has
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:59:a *least* element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]), so $j(a)$ is determined by
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:60:$a$ and $s$ alone.
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:61:
items/def-countable.md:43:## Definition
items/def-countable.md:44:
items/def-countable.md:45:Recall that a natural number is a von Neumann natural
items/def-countable.md:46:([[def-natural-numbers]]): $0 = \varnothing$ and $\sigma(n) = n \cup \{n\}$, so
items/def-countable.md:47:that
items/def-countable.md:48:
items/def-countable.md:49:$$n = \{\, m \in \mathbb{N} : m < n \,\} = \{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$$
items/def-countable.md:50:
items/def-countable.md:51:is itself the set of its predecessors. Here $<$ is the order of
items/def-countable.md:52:[[def-nat-order]], which is defined additively, so the displayed identity is a
items/def-countable.md:53:theorem and not a convention: it is [[lem-nat-order-is-membership]], proved
items/def-countable.md:54:immediately above. Let $A$ be a set, and let $\approx$ be equinumerosity
items/def-countable.md:55:([[def-equinumerous]]).
items/def-countable.md:56:
items/def-countable.md:57:- $A$ is **finite** if $A \approx n$ for some $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
items/def-countable.md:58:- $A$ is **countably infinite** if $A \approx \mathbb{N}$.
items/def-countable.md:59:- $A$ is **at most countable** if it is finite or countably infinite.
items/def-countable.md:60:- $A$ is **uncountable** if it is not at most countable.
items/def-countable.md:61:
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:39:## Statement
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:40:
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:41:Let $X$ be a topological space and let $C(x)$ be the connected component of
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:42:$x \in X$ ([[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]]). Then:
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:43:
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:44:1. **Maximality.** $C(x)$ is connected, contains $x$, and contains every
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:45:   connected subset of $X$ that contains $x$. So the components are exactly the
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:46:   maximal connected subsets of $X$: a connected $A \subseteq X$ is a component
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:47:   if and only if no connected subset of $X$ properly contains $A$ — except in
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:48:   the empty space, where $\varnothing$ is vacuously maximal and yet is not a
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:49:   component, there being no points; for nonempty $X$ no exception is needed,
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:50:   since $\varnothing$ is properly contained in a connected singleton and so is
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:51:   never maximal.
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:52:2. **Partition.** For $x, y \in X$, either $C(x) = C(y)$ or
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:53:   $C(x) \cap C(y) = \varnothing$; every point lies in its own component; and
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:54:   $X = \bigcup_{x \in X} C(x)$. So the components are nonempty, pairwise
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:55:   disjoint, and cover $X$.
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:56:3. **Closedness.** Every component is closed in $X$.
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:57:
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:58:**Components need not be open**, and no clause above says they are. Openness of
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:59:the components is a genuine extra hypothesis on $X$, taken up later on this page
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:60:under the name local connectedness.
items/thm-components-partition-and-are-closed.md:61:
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:39:## Statement
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:40:
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:41:Let $X$ be a topological space, with subsets carrying the subspace topology
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:42:([[def-subspace-topology-top]]). Then:
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:43:
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:44:1. $X$ is locally connected ([[def-locally-connected]]) **if and only if** for
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:45:   every open $U \subseteq X$ every component of the space $U$
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:46:   ([[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]]) is open in $X$.
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:47:2. If $X$ is locally connected then every component of $X$ is **clopen**.
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:48:3. The same statement with "path-connected" throughout: $X$ is locally
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:49:   path-connected if and only if for every open $U \subseteq X$ every path
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:50:   component of the space $U$ is open in $X$.
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:51:
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:52:In claim 1 "open in $X$" and "open in $U$" say the same thing, $U$ being open in
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:53:$X$ ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]); the statement is written with the ambient
items/thm-locally-connected-iff-components-of-open-sets-are-open.md:54:form because that is how it is used.
