items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:46:## Statement
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:47:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:48:Let $R = \omega_1 \times [0,1)$ be the closed long ray with its lexicographic
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:49:order and its order topology ([[def-the-long-line]]). Then:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:50:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:51:1. **$R$ is a linear continuum**
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:52:   ([[def-order-topology-on-a-linearly-ordered-set]]): it has at least two
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:53:   elements, it is order-dense, and it has the least upper bound property.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:54:2. **$R$ is connected**, and so is every order-convex subset of $R$; in
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:55:   particular every initial segment
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:56:   $[0_R, x] = \{\, y \in R : y \le x \,\}$ is connected.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:57:3. **Assuming the Axiom of Countable Choice $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$**
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:58:   ([[def-countable-choice]]): every at most countable subset of $R$
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:59:   ([[def-countable]]) has an upper bound in $R$; so no at most countable subset
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:60:   of $R$ is unbounded above.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:61:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:62:Claims 1 and 2 are theorems of ZF. Claim 3 carries the hypothesis because it is
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:63:inherited whole from [[thm-countable-subsets-of-omega-one-are-bounded]], whose
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:64:own statement carries it, and it is spent at exactly one step below.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:65:
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:66:**Nothing here says $R$ is path-connected**, and this proof gives no path
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:67:between two of its points; that question needs an order isomorphism of each
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:68:initial segment with $[0,1]$, which is not constructed on this page.
items/thm-the-long-line-is-a-connected-linear-continuum.md:69:
items/def-countable-choice.md:42:## Definition
items/def-countable-choice.md:43:
items/def-countable-choice.md:44:The **Axiom of Countable Choice**, written $\mathrm{AC}_\omega$, is the following
items/def-countable-choice.md:45:statement.
items/def-countable-choice.md:46:
items/def-countable-choice.md:47:> For every family $(X_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of nonempty sets indexed by
items/def-countable-choice.md:48:> $\mathbb{N}$ there is a function $f$ with domain $\mathbb{N}$ such that
items/def-countable-choice.md:49:> $f(n) \in X_n$ for every $n \in \mathbb{N}$.
items/def-countable-choice.md:50:
items/def-countable-choice.md:51:Equivalently, in the vocabulary of [[def-choice-function]]: every at most
items/def-countable-choice.md:52:countable family of nonempty sets ([[def-countable]]) has a choice function.
items/def-countable-choice.md:53:

