items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:30:## Statement
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:31:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:32:**(a) The alephs, in ZF.** There is **exactly one** class operation
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:33:$\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$, defined at every ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]) and
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:34:given by a formula, satisfying
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:35:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:36:$$\aleph_0 = \omega, \qquad \aleph_{\alpha + 1} = \aleph(\aleph_\alpha), \qquad \aleph_\lambda = \bigcup\{\, \aleph_\alpha : \alpha \in \lambda \,\} \ \ (\lambda \text{ a limit ordinal}),$$
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:37:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:38:where $\aleph(\cdot)$ is the Hartogs number ([[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]])
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:39:and $\alpha + 1 = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$.
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:40:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:41:**(b)** Every $\aleph_\alpha$ is an infinite cardinal ([[def-cardinal]]); the
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:42:operation is **strictly increasing**, $\alpha \in \beta \Rightarrow
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:43:\aleph_\alpha \in \aleph_\beta$; and it is **continuous at limits**, which is the
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:44:third clause read as a supremum.
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:45:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:46:**(c)** $\alpha \le \aleph_\alpha$ for every ordinal $\alpha$.
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:47:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:48:**(d) The beths, assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). There
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:49:is exactly one class operation $\alpha \mapsto \beth_\alpha$, defined at every
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:50:ordinal, with
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:51:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:52:$$\beth_0 = \omega, \qquad \beth_{\alpha + 1} = 2^{\beth_\alpha}, \qquad \beth_\lambda = \bigcup\{\, \beth_\alpha : \alpha \in \lambda \,\} \ \ (\lambda \text{ a limit ordinal}),$$
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:53:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:54:and it too takes infinite cardinal values, is strictly increasing, and is
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:55:continuous at limits.
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:56:
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:57:Like [[lem-recursion-on-the-ordinals]] these are **theorem schemas**: an instance
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:58:for each of the defining formulas. The aleph half uses Replacement and no choice; the
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:59:beth half needs the Axiom of Choice, and needs it only because $2^{\kappa}$ does
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:60:([[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]]).
items/cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined.md:61:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:41:## Definition
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:42:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:43:**The successor cardinal.** For a cardinal $\kappa$ ([[def-cardinal]]) write
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:44:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:45:$$\kappa^{+} \;:=\; \aleph(\kappa),$$
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:46:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:47:the Hartogs number of $\kappa$ ([[thm-hartogs]]). By
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:48:[[lem-successor-cardinal-exists]] this is the least cardinal strictly above
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:49:$\kappa$, and its existence is a theorem of ZF.
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:50:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:51:> **Notation rule, in force on this page and its companion.** The superscript
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:52:> $+$ means the **successor cardinal** only on a cardinal letter
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:53:> $\kappa, \lambda, \mu$ or an aleph. On an ordinal letter
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:54:> $\alpha, \beta, \gamma, \xi, \eta$ the superscript $+$ keeps its published
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:55:> meaning, the **ordinal successor** $\alpha^{+} = \alpha \cup \{\alpha\}$ of
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:56:> [[def-ordinal]]. The two never agree on an infinite cardinal: $\kappa \cup \{\kappa\}$
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:57:> is a successor ordinal and therefore not a cardinal at all, while $\kappa^{+}$
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:58:> is much larger. To keep the reader out of the collision, everything below
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:59:> writes $\alpha + 1$ for the ordinal successor and reserves $\kappa^{+}$ for the
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:60:> cardinal one.
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:61:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:62:**The alephs.** By [[cor-the-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies-are-well-defined]] there
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:63:is exactly one operation $\alpha \mapsto \aleph_\alpha$, defined at every ordinal
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:64:([[def-ordinal]]), with
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:65:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:66:$$\aleph_0 = \omega, \qquad \aleph_{\alpha+1} = \aleph_\alpha^{+}, \qquad \aleph_\lambda = \sup\{\, \aleph_\alpha : \alpha \in \lambda \,\} \ \ (\lambda \text{ a limit ordinal}),$$
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:67:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:68:the limit clause being taken over limit ordinals in the sense of
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:69:[[def-limit-ordinal]], and $\sup$ being $\bigcup$ applied to a set of ordinals.
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:70:Every $\aleph_\alpha$ is an infinite cardinal, the operation is strictly
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:71:increasing, and $\alpha \le \aleph_\alpha$; all of this is that corollary, and all
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:72:of it is ZF.
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:73:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:74:**The beths**, assuming the Axiom of Choice, are the parallel operation
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:75:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:76:$$\beth_0 = \omega, \qquad \beth_{\alpha+1} = 2^{\beth_\alpha}, \qquad \beth_\lambda = \sup\{\, \beth_\alpha : \alpha \in \lambda \,\} \ \ (\lambda \text{ a limit ordinal}),$$
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:77:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:78:with $2^{\kappa}$ the cardinal power of [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]].
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:79:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:80:**Successor and limit cardinals.** An infinite cardinal $\kappa$ is a
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:81:**successor cardinal** when $\kappa = \lambda^{+}$ for some infinite cardinal
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:82:$\lambda$, and a **limit cardinal** otherwise. So $\aleph_{\alpha+1}$ is a
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:83:successor cardinal for every $\alpha$, and $\aleph_0 = \omega$ is a limit
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:84:cardinal, there being no infinite cardinal below it.
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:85:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:86:**The two identifications.**
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:87:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:88:$$\aleph_0 = \omega, \qquad \aleph_1 = \omega_1 .$$
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:89:
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:90:The first is the base clause. The second holds because
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:91:$\aleph_1 = \aleph_0^{+} = \aleph(\omega)$, and $\aleph(\omega)$ is by definition
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:92:the first uncountable ordinal $\omega_1$ ([[def-first-uncountable-ordinal]]);
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:93:[[thm-omega-one-is-the-least-uncountable-ordinal]] independently confirms that
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:94:$\omega_1$ is a cardinal, is uncountable ([[def-countable]]), and has every
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:95:ordinal below it at most countable, which is the same thing said in the language
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:96:of the ordinal development.
items/def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies.md:97:
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:40:## Statement
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:41:
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:42:Work in ZF; no choice principle is used. For a set $A$ let $\aleph(A)$ be its
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:43:Hartogs number ([[thm-hartogs]]), the least ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]) admitting
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:44:no injection into $A$. Then:
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:45:
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:46:**(a)** $\aleph(A)$ is a cardinal ([[def-cardinal]]), for **every** set $A$,
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:47:well-orderable or not.
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:48:
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:49:**(b)** If $\kappa$ is a cardinal then $\kappa < \aleph(\kappa)$, and every
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:50:cardinal $\lambda$ with $\kappa < \lambda$ satisfies $\aleph(\kappa) \le \lambda$.
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:51:So $\aleph(\kappa)$ is the **least cardinal strictly above $\kappa$**.
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:52:
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:53:**(c)** If $\kappa$ is an infinite cardinal then so is $\aleph(\kappa)$.
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:54:
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:55:**What this supplies, and what it does not.** It gives a successor operation on
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:56:cardinals in ZF alone: there is always a next one, and it is definable. It says
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:57:nothing about how *large* the next cardinal is compared with $2^{\kappa}$; that
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:58:comparison is not decided by the axioms in use here.
items/lem-successor-cardinal-exists.md:59:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:42:## Statement
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:43:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:44:Call a set $X$ **well-orderable** when some relation well-orders it
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:45:([[def-well-order]]). Work in ZF, with no choice principle. Then:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:46:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:47:**(a)** $X$ is well-orderable if and only if $X \approx \alpha$
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:48:([[def-equinumerous]]) for some ordinal $\alpha$ ([[def-ordinal]]).
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:49:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:50:**(b)** If $X$ is well-orderable there is a **least** ordinal equinumerous with
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:51:$X$. It is written $\lvert X \rvert$ and called the **cardinality** of $X$.
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:52:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:53:**(c)** $\lvert X \rvert$ is a cardinal ([[def-cardinal]]).
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:54:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:55:**(d)** If $X \approx Y$ and $X$ is well-orderable, then $Y$ is well-orderable
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:56:and $\lvert Y \rvert = \lvert X \rvert$.
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:57:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:58:**(e)** $\lvert \alpha \rvert \le \alpha$ for every ordinal $\alpha$, and
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:59:$\lvert \alpha \rvert = \alpha$ exactly when $\alpha$ is a cardinal.
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:60:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:61:Assuming the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every set is
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:62:well-orderable ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]), so $\lvert X \rvert$ is then
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:63:defined for every set and is exactly the cardinality of [[def-cardinal]].
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:64:
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:65:**Why this item exists.** [[def-cardinal]] introduces $\lvert X \rvert$ under the
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:66:hypothesis "Assume the Axiom of Choice", and it needs that hypothesis only to
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:67:know that $X$ carries a well-order at all. Everything below is about
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:68:well-orderable sets and is a theorem of ZF, which is what makes it possible to
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:69:state Hessenberg's theorem and Tarski's theorem, one of which is choice-free and
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:70:the other of which is precisely about the gap between ZF and ZFC.
items/lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set.md:71:
items/cor-ac-iff-well-ordering.md:43:## Statement
items/cor-ac-iff-well-ordering.md:44:
items/cor-ac-iff-well-ordering.md:45:Over ZF the following three statements are equivalent:
items/cor-ac-iff-well-ordering.md:46:
items/cor-ac-iff-well-ordering.md:47:**(1)** the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]);
items/cor-ac-iff-well-ordering.md:48:
