items/thm-konig.md:40:## Statement
items/thm-konig.md:41:
items/thm-konig.md:42:**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $I$ be a set and
items/thm-konig.md:43:let $(\kappa_i)_{i \in I}$ and $(\lambda_i)_{i \in I}$ be families of cardinals
items/thm-konig.md:44:([[def-cardinal]]) with
items/thm-konig.md:45:
items/thm-konig.md:46:$$\kappa_i < \lambda_i \qquad \text{for every } i \in I .$$
items/thm-konig.md:47:
items/thm-konig.md:48:Then
items/thm-konig.md:49:
items/thm-konig.md:50:$$\sum_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;<\; \prod_{i \in I} \lambda_i$$
items/thm-konig.md:51:
items/thm-konig.md:52:([[def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product]]).
items/thm-konig.md:53:
items/thm-konig.md:54:**The hypothesis is named in the statement, not only in the facts**, and it is
items/thm-konig.md:55:spent twice: once in the definition of the two sides, which are cardinalities of
items/thm-konig.md:56:sets ZF does not well-order, and once in the diagonal step of the proof, which
items/thm-konig.md:57:selects an omitted value in each coordinate at the same time.
items/thm-konig.md:58:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:39:## Definition
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:40:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:41:**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Let $I$ be a set and
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:42:$(\kappa_i)_{i \in I}$ a family of cardinals ([[def-cardinal]]), that is, a
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:43:function on $I$ whose value at $i$ is the cardinal $\kappa_i$. Put
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:44:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:45:$$\textstyle\bigsqcup_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;:=\; \bigcup_{i \in I} \big(\{i\} \times \kappa_i\big), \qquad \prod^{\mathrm{set}}_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;:=\; \{\, f : f \text{ is a function on } I \text{ with } f(i) \in \kappa_i \text{ for all } i \in I \,\},$$
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:46:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:47:both sets by Replacement, Union and Power Set. The **sum** and **product** of the
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:48:family are their cardinalities:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:49:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:50:$$\sum_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;:=\; \Big\lvert \textstyle\bigsqcup_{i \in I} \kappa_i \Big\rvert, \qquad \prod_{i \in I} \kappa_i \;:=\; \Big\lvert \prod^{\mathrm{set}}_{i \in I} \kappa_i \Big\rvert .$$
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:51:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:52:**Why the hypothesis is in the definition.** Both right-hand sides are
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:53:cardinalities of sets that ZF does not well-order. Under the Axiom of Choice
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:54:every set is well-orderable ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]) and both values exist
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:55:([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]). Nothing else is being assumed: the
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:56:two sets themselves are constructed in ZF, and the family
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:57:$(\kappa_i)_{i \in I}$ is a *function*, so no representative is selected.
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:58:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:59:**The finite cases are the operations already defined.** Take $I = 2 = \{0,1\}$.
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:60:Then $\bigsqcup_{i \in 2} \kappa_i = (\{0\} \times \kappa_0) \cup (\{1\} \times \kappa_1)
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:61:= \kappa_0 \sqcup \kappa_1$ literally, so
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:62:$\sum_{i \in 2} \kappa_i = \kappa_0 \oplus \kappa_1$
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:63:([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]); and $f \mapsto (f(0), f(1))$ is a bijection from
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:64:$\prod^{\mathrm{set}}_{i \in 2} \kappa_i$ onto $\kappa_0 \times \kappa_1$, with
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:65:inverse $(a,b) \mapsto \{(0,a),(1,b)\}$, so
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:66:$\prod_{i \in 2} \kappa_i = \kappa_0 \otimes \kappa_1$ by
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:67:[[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]].
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:68:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:69:**A constant family recovers $\otimes$ and exponentiation.** If $\kappa_i = \kappa$
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:70:for every $i \in I$ and $\lambda = \lvert I \rvert$, then
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:71:$\bigsqcup_{i \in I} \kappa = I \times \kappa$ and
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:72:$\prod^{\mathrm{set}}_{i \in I} \kappa = {}^{I}\kappa$, so
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:73:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:74:$$\sum_{i \in I} \kappa = \lambda \otimes \kappa, \qquad \prod_{i \in I} \kappa = \kappa^{\lambda},$$
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:75:
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:76:by the transport clause of [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]] together with
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:77:[[lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined]] and
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:78:[[def-injection-surjection-bijection]].
items/def-infinite-cardinal-sum-and-product.md:79:
items/def-cofinality.md:39:## Definition
items/def-cofinality.md:40:
items/def-cofinality.md:41:Let $\alpha$ be an ordinal ([[def-ordinal]]). The **cofinality** of $\alpha$ is
items/def-cofinality.md:42:
items/def-cofinality.md:43:$$\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \;:=\; \text{the least ordinal } \beta \text{ for which some } f : \beta \to \alpha \text{ has cofinal range},$$
items/def-cofinality.md:44:
items/def-cofinality.md:45:cofinal range meaning that $f[\beta]$ is a cofinal subset of $\alpha$
items/def-cofinality.md:46:([[def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal]]): every $\zeta \in \alpha$ satisfies
items/def-cofinality.md:47:$\zeta \le f(\xi)$ for some $\xi \in \beta$. That such a least ordinal exists, and
items/def-cofinality.md:48:that a witnessing map of that length may be taken strictly increasing, is
items/def-cofinality.md:49:[[lem-cofinality-is-well-defined]], and both are theorems of ZF. So
items/def-cofinality.md:50:$\operatorname{cf}$ is defined at every ordinal, without any choice principle.
items/def-cofinality.md:51:
items/def-cofinality.md:52:**Regular and singular.** An **infinite cardinal** $\kappa$
items/def-cofinality.md:53:([[def-cardinal]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]) is
items/def-cofinality.md:54:
items/def-cofinality.md:55:- **regular** when $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) = \kappa$;
items/def-cofinality.md:56:- **singular** when $\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) \ne \kappa$.
items/def-cofinality.md:57:
items/def-cofinality.md:58:The two cases are exhaustive by definition, and by
items/def-cofinality.md:59:[[thm-cofinality-basics]] singular means exactly
items/def-cofinality.md:60:$\operatorname{cf}(\kappa) < \kappa$, since $\operatorname{cf}(\alpha) \le \alpha$
items/def-cofinality.md:61:always holds.
items/def-cofinality.md:62:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:40:## Statement
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:41:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:42:Let $\kappa$, $\lambda$, $\mu$ be cardinals ([[def-cardinal]]) and let
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:43:$\oplus$, $\otimes$, $\kappa^{\lambda}$ be as in [[def-cardinal-arithmetic]].
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:44:Clauses (a) to (e) are theorems of ZF; the clauses naming an exponential hold
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:45:whenever those exponentials are defined, in particular under the Axiom of Choice
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:46:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:47:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:48:**(a) Comparison.** $\kappa \le \lambda$ if and only if $\kappa \preceq \lambda$,
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:49:that is, if and only if there is an injection $\kappa \to \lambda$
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:50:([[def-equinumerous]]). More generally, if $A$ and $B$ are well-orderable and
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:51:$A \preceq B$ then $\lvert A \rvert \le \lvert B \rvert$.
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:52:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:53:**(b) Commutativity and associativity.**
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:54:$\kappa \oplus \lambda = \lambda \oplus \kappa$,
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:55:$\kappa \otimes \lambda = \lambda \otimes \kappa$,
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:56:$(\kappa \oplus \lambda) \oplus \mu = \kappa \oplus (\lambda \oplus \mu)$ and
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:57:$(\kappa \otimes \lambda) \otimes \mu = \kappa \otimes (\lambda \otimes \mu)$.
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:58:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:59:**(c) Distributivity.**
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:60:$\kappa \otimes (\lambda \oplus \mu) = (\kappa \otimes \lambda) \oplus (\kappa \otimes \mu)$.
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:61:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:62:**(d) Units.** $\kappa \oplus 0 = \kappa$, $\kappa \otimes 0 = 0$,
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:63:$\kappa \otimes 1 = \kappa$, and $\kappa^{0} = 1$, $\kappa^{1} = \kappa$,
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:64:$1^{\kappa} = 1$, together with $0^{\kappa} = 0$ for $\kappa \ne 0$. The four
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:65:exponential unit laws need no choice principle, because the function sets they
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:66:count are empty, a singleton, or a copy of $\kappa$.
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:67:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:68:**(e) Monotonicity.** If $\kappa \le \lambda$ then
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:69:$\kappa \oplus \mu \le \lambda \oplus \mu$ and
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:70:$\kappa \otimes \mu \le \lambda \otimes \mu$; and
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:71:$\kappa^{\mu} \le \lambda^{\mu}$, and $\mu^{\kappa} \le \mu^{\lambda}$ provided
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:72:$\mu \ne 0$.
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:73:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:74:**(f) The two exponent laws.**
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:75:$\kappa^{\lambda \oplus \mu} = \kappa^{\lambda} \otimes \kappa^{\mu}$ and
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:76:$(\kappa^{\lambda})^{\mu} = \kappa^{\lambda \otimes \mu}$.
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:77:
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:78:**Each clause is an equality or an inequality of cardinals**, not merely of sizes: each side is
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:79:an ordinal, and the claim is that the two ordinals are the same.
items/lem-cardinal-arithmetic-basic-laws.md:80:
items/thm-hessenberg.md:42:## Statement
items/thm-hessenberg.md:43:
items/thm-hessenberg.md:44:Let $\kappa$ be an **infinite cardinal**, that is a cardinal
items/thm-hessenberg.md:45:([[def-cardinal]]) with $\omega \le \kappa$. Then
items/thm-hessenberg.md:46:
items/thm-hessenberg.md:47:$$\kappa \otimes \kappa = \kappa, \qquad \text{equivalently} \qquad \lvert \kappa \times \kappa \rvert = \kappa$$
items/thm-hessenberg.md:48:
items/thm-hessenberg.md:49:([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]).
items/thm-hessenberg.md:50:
items/thm-hessenberg.md:51:**This is a theorem of ZF and uses no choice principle.** The well-order that
items/thm-hessenberg.md:52:carries the proof is written down from the ordinal order on $\kappa$; nothing is
items/thm-hessenberg.md:53:selected anywhere. That matters for this page: Hessenberg's theorem is exactly
items/thm-hessenberg.md:54:the part of "an infinite set is the same size as its square" that survives
items/thm-hessenberg.md:55:without choice.
items/thm-hessenberg.md:56:
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:30:## Statement
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:31:
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:32:**Assume the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]), so that every set has a
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:33:cardinality ([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]],
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:34:[[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]). Let $\kappa$ be a cardinal
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:35:([[def-cardinal]]) and read $2 = \{0,1\}$ as a cardinal. Then:
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:36:
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:37:**(a)** $2^{\kappa} = \lvert \mathcal{P}(\kappa) \rvert$
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:38:([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]]), and more generally
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:39:$2^{\lvert A \rvert} = \lvert \mathcal{P}(A) \rvert$ for every set $A$;
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:40:
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:41:**(b)** $\kappa < 2^{\kappa}$.
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:42:
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:43:Clause (b) is [[thm-cantor-powerset]] transcribed into cardinal arithmetic. The
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:44:underlying combinatorial fact — that there is no surjection $A \to \mathcal{P}(A)$
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:45:— is a theorem of ZF and needs no choice at all; what the Axiom of Choice buys
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:46:here is only the right to write $\lvert \mathcal{P}(A) \rvert$ and $2^{\kappa}$
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:47:as cardinals in the first place.
items/thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor.md:48:
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:46:## Statement
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:47:
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:48:Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). Then **every set $X$ can
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:49:be well ordered**: there is a relation on $X$ making it a well-ordered set
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:50:([[def-well-order]]).
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:51:
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:52:The Axiom of Choice is used only inside [[thm-zorn]], and nowhere else in the
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:53:argument below.
items/thm-well-ordering-theorem.md:54:
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:41:## Definition
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:42:
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:43:The **Axiom of Choice** (AC) is the following statement.
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:44:
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:45:> Every family of nonempty sets has a choice function
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:46:> ([[def-choice-function]]).
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:47:
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:48:Written out: for every set $\mathcal{F}$ all of whose members are nonempty,
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:49:there exists a function $g$ with domain $\mathcal{F}$ satisfying $g(S) \in S$ for
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:50:all $S \in \mathcal{F}$.
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:51:
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:52:An equivalent formulation, immediate from the definition of the Cartesian
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:53:product, is that a product of nonempty sets is nonempty: if $X_i \ne \emptyset$
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:54:for every $i \in I$, then $\prod_{i \in I} X_i \ne \emptyset$.
items/def-axiom-of-choice.md:55:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:30:## Statement
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:31:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:32:Let $\operatorname{cf}$, regular and singular be as in [[def-cofinality]], and
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:33:let $\aleph_\alpha$ be as in [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]. Then:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:34:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:35:**(a) In ZF.** $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_0) = \aleph_0$, so $\aleph_0$ is
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:36:regular.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:37:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:38:**(b) Assuming the Axiom of Choice** ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]).
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:39:$\aleph_{\alpha+1}$ is regular for every ordinal $\alpha$.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:40:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:41:**(c) In ZF.** $\operatorname{cf}(\aleph_\omega) = \aleph_0$, and
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:42:$\aleph_0 < \aleph_\omega$, so $\aleph_\omega$ is **singular**.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:43:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:44:**(d) Assuming the Axiom of Choice.** Every infinite cardinal below
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:45:$\aleph_\omega$ is regular, so $\aleph_\omega$ is the least singular infinite
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:46:cardinal.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:47:
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:48:**Clause (b) is where the Axiom of Choice becomes indispensable, and the
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:49:hypothesis is not decoration.** The proof spends it once, to select an injection
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:50:$g(\xi) \to \aleph_\alpha$ for each $\xi$ below the cofinality, and there is no
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:51:canonical such family to fall back on: the sets $g(\xi)$ are ordinals, but the
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:52:injections are not determined by them. Clauses (a), (c) and the classification
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:53:half of (d) are choice free.
items/thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md:54:
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:40:## Statement
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:41:
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:42:**FALSE.** Assume the Axiom of Choice ([[def-axiom-of-choice]]). The continuum
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:43:has cardinality $\aleph_\omega$:
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:44:
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:45:$$2^{\aleph_0} = \aleph_\omega$$
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:46:
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:47:([[def-cardinal-arithmetic]], [[def-aleph-and-beth-hierarchies]]).
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:48:
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:49:The claim is plausible because ZFC really does leave the value of $2^{\aleph_0}$
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:50:open over a wide range of alephs, and $\aleph_\omega$ is a natural-looking
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:51:candidate: it is above $\aleph_0$, as [[thm-cardinal-power-set-and-cantor]]
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:52:requires, and it is not a successor, so no obvious counting argument seems to
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:53:touch it. Nevertheless ZFC refutes it outright, and the refutation is short.
items/fs-the-continuum-has-cardinality-aleph-omega.md:54:

