items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:31:## Statement
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:32:
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:33:Let $D$ be the set of sequences $a : \mathbb{N} \to \{0,2\}$
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:34:([[def-sequence]]), the two values being the real numbers $0$ and $2$. For
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:35:$a \in D$ the series $\sum_{k \ge 0} a_k 3^{-k-1}$ converges ([[def-series]]);
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:36:write
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:37:
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:38:$$\Phi(a) \;:=\; \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} a_k 3^{-k-1} .$$
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:39:
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:40:Then, with $C$ and $(C_n)$ as in [[def-cantor-set]]:
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:41:
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:42:1. $\Phi(a) \in [0,1]$ for every $a \in D$, and $C = \{\, \Phi(a) : a \in D \,\}$;
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:43:2. $\Phi$ is injective, so $\Phi$ is a bijection from $D$ onto $C$
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:44:   ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]);
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:45:3. consequently $b \mapsto \Phi\big((2 b_k)_k\big)$ is a bijection from
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:46:   $\{0,1\}^{\mathbb{N}}$, the set of sequences with values in $\{0,1\}$, onto
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:47:   $C$;
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:48:4. $C = \tfrac13 C \cup \big(\tfrac23 + \tfrac13 C\big)$, and the two sets on the
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:49:   right are disjoint.
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:50:
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:51:**On the indexing.** The digit $a_k$ carries the weight $3^{-k-1}$, so the
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:52:series starts at $k = 0$ with the term $a_0/3$; written with the classical
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:53:$1$-based index it reads $\sum_{k \ge 1} a_k 3^{-k}$, which is the form in the
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:54:title. Sequences in this library are functions on $\mathbb{N}$ and $\mathbb{N}$
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:55:contains $0$ ([[def-sequence]]), so the $0$-based form is the one used
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:56:throughout the proof.
items/thm-cantor-set-ternary-description.md:57:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:30:## Statement
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:31:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:32:For sets $A$ and $B$ write
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:33:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:34:$$A \sqcup B := (\{0\} \times A) \cup (\{1\} \times B), \qquad {}^{B}A := \{\, h : h \text{ is a function } B \to A \,\},$$
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:35:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:36:so $A \sqcup B$ is the **disjoint union**, made disjoint by tagging, and
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:37:${}^{B}A$ is the set of all functions from $B$ to $A$. Work in ZF. Then:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:38:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:39:**(a) Representative independence.** If $A \approx A'$ and $B \approx B'$
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:40:([[def-equinumerous]]) then
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:41:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:42:$$A \sqcup B \approx A' \sqcup B', \qquad A \times B \approx A' \times B', \qquad {}^{B}A \approx {}^{B'}A'.$$
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:43:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:44:**(b) Power sets.** If $A \approx B$ then $\mathcal{P}(A) \approx \mathcal{P}(B)$.
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:45:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:46:**(c) Two operations are choice-free.** For ordinals $\alpha$ and $\beta$
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:47:([[def-ordinal]]) the sets $\alpha \sqcup \beta$ and $\alpha \times \beta$ carry
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:48:**explicitly defined** well-orders ([[def-well-order]]), so each is equinumerous
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:49:with an ordinal and each has a cardinality
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:50:$\lvert \alpha \sqcup \beta \rvert$, $\lvert \alpha \times \beta \rvert$
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:51:in ZF ([[lem-cardinality-of-a-well-orderable-set]]).
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:52:
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:53:**(d) The third is not.** Nothing here well-orders ${}^{\beta}\alpha$, and no
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:54:argument on this page does. Assuming the Axiom of Choice
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:55:([[def-axiom-of-choice]]) every set is well-orderable
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:56:([[thm-well-ordering-theorem]]) and ${}^{\beta}\alpha$ has a cardinality like any
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:57:other set; that is where cardinal exponentiation gets its hypothesis.
items/lem-cardinal-operations-are-well-defined.md:58:
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:42:## Statement
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:43:
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:44:Let $A$ and $B$ be sets with $A \preceq B$ and $B \preceq A$
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:45:([[def-equinumerous]]). Then $A \approx B$.
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:46:
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:47:Equivalently: if there is an injection $A \to B$ and an injection $B \to A$, then
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:48:there is a bijection $A \to B$ ([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:49:
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:50:**The proof uses no choice principle.** The bijection is written down explicitly
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:51:from the two given injections, and the only "selections" it makes are of the
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:52:unique preimage of a point under an injection, which is determined, not chosen.
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:53:The single infinite construction is an application of the recursion theorem
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:54:([[thm-recursion]]), whose data are a set, a starting point and one function.
items/thm-schroder-bernstein.md:55:
items/lem-of-q-dense.md:30:## Statement
items/lem-of-q-dense.md:31:
items/lem-of-q-dense.md:32:Let $F$ be an Archimedean ordered field ([[def-archimedean-field]]) and let
items/lem-of-q-dense.md:33:$\iota : \mathbb{Q} \to F$ be the canonical embedding ([[lem-of-q-embeds]]). Then
items/lem-of-q-dense.md:34:$\iota(\mathbb{Q})$ is dense in $F$: for any $x < y$ in $F$ there is a rational
items/lem-of-q-dense.md:35:$q$ with $x < \iota(q) < y$.
items/lem-of-q-dense.md:36:
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:44:## Statement
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:45:
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:46:$\mathbb{Q} \approx \mathbb{N}$ ([[def-equinumerous]]): the rationals are
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:47:countably infinite ([[def-countable]]).
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:48:
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:49:**No choice principle is used.** The one place where a reader expects a choice,
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:50:"pick a representative $a/b$ of each rational", is exactly where
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:51:[[lem-rat-positive-denominator]] applies: every rational *has* a representative
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:52:with positive denominator, so the map $(a,b) \mapsto [(a,b)]$ defined on
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:53:$\mathbb{Z} \times \mathbb{Z}_{>0}$ is already **surjective** onto $\mathbb{Q}$,
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:54:and countability follows from a surjection without ever selecting a
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:55:representative. The same device handles $\mathbb{Z}$, which is a surjective image
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:56:of $\mathbb{N} \times \mathbb{N}$ by construction ([[def-integers]]).
items/thm-rationals-countable.md:57:

