items/def-product-topology.md:37:## Definition
items/def-product-topology.md:38:
items/def-product-topology.md:39:**The product set.** Let $I$ be a set and let $X_i$ be a set for each $i \in I$.
items/def-product-topology.md:40:The **product** is
items/def-product-topology.md:41:
items/def-product-topology.md:42:$$\prod_{i \in I} X_i \;:=\; \Big\{\, x : x \text{ is a function with domain } I \text{ and } x(i) \in X_i \text{ for every } i \in I \,\Big\},$$
items/def-product-topology.md:43:
items/def-product-topology.md:44:and we write $x_i := x(i)$, the $i$-th **coordinate** of $x$. Two elements of the
items/def-product-topology.md:45:product are equal exactly when they agree at every index, functions being equal
items/def-product-topology.md:46:when they have the same domain and the same values. For $j \in I$ the $j$-th
items/def-product-topology.md:47:**projection** is
items/def-product-topology.md:48:
items/def-product-topology.md:49:$$\pi_j : \prod_{i \in I} X_i \to X_j, \qquad \pi_j(x) := x_j .$$
items/def-product-topology.md:50:
items/def-product-topology.md:51:**Notation for a finite product.** For $I = n$ a natural number, which is the set
items/def-product-topology.md:52:$\{0, 1, \dots, n-1\}$ of its predecessors, an element of $\prod_{k<n} X_k$ is a
items/def-product-topology.md:53:function on $n$ and we write it $(x_0, \dots, x_{n-1})$. In particular $I = 2$
items/def-product-topology.md:54:gives the **binary product**, written $X \times Y$ for $\prod_{i<2} X_i$ with
items/def-product-topology.md:55:$X_0 = X$ and $X_1 = Y$, whose elements are written $(u,v)$ for the function
items/def-product-topology.md:56:$0 \mapsto u$, $1 \mapsto v$. This is the only meaning the symbol $X \times Y$
items/def-product-topology.md:57:carries on this page.
items/def-product-topology.md:58:
items/def-product-topology.md:59:**Two facts about when the product is nonempty, stated because they are used and
items/def-product-topology.md:60:because they cost something.** If some $X_{i_0}$ is empty then the product is
items/def-product-topology.md:61:empty, since no function can take a value in $X_{i_0}$. Conversely, suppose every
items/def-product-topology.md:62:$X_i$ is nonempty.
items/def-product-topology.md:63:
items/def-product-topology.md:64:- For $I = n$ a natural number, the product is nonempty, and this is a theorem of
items/def-product-topology.md:65:  ZF: [[lem-finite-choice]] applied to the function $i \mapsto X_i$ on $n$
items/def-product-topology.md:66:  supplies a choice function $g$ for the family of values, and
items/def-product-topology.md:67:  $x(i) := g(X_i)$ defines a member of $\prod_{k<n} X_k$.
items/def-product-topology.md:68:- For an arbitrary $I$ the assertion "$\prod_{i \in I} X_i \ne \varnothing$
items/def-product-topology.md:69:  whenever every $X_i$ is nonempty" **is** the Axiom of Choice: it is the
items/def-product-topology.md:70:  formulation recorded in [[def-axiom-of-choice]], and the choice function of
items/def-product-topology.md:71:  [[def-choice-function]] is exactly a point of the product of a family by
items/def-product-topology.md:72:  itself. Every use of it below is flagged at the step that spends it.
items/def-product-topology.md:73:
items/def-product-topology.md:74:**The box topology.** Now let each $X_i$ carry a topology $\mathcal{T}_i$
items/def-product-topology.md:75:([[def-topological-space]]). Put
items/def-product-topology.md:76:
items/def-product-topology.md:77:$$\mathcal{R} \;:=\; \Big\{\, \prod_{i \in I} U_i \;:\; U_i \in \mathcal{T}_i \text{ for every } i \in I \,\Big\},$$
items/def-product-topology.md:78:
items/def-product-topology.md:79:the family of **boxes**. $\mathcal{R}$ is a basis for a topology
items/def-product-topology.md:80:([[thm-basis-criterion]]): it contains $\prod_i X_i$, so it covers the product,
items/def-product-topology.md:81:and it is closed under binary intersections, since
items/def-product-topology.md:82:
items/def-product-topology.md:83:$$\Big(\prod_i U_i\Big) \cap \Big(\prod_i V_i\Big) = \prod_i (U_i \cap V_i)$$
items/def-product-topology.md:84:
items/def-product-topology.md:85:and each $U_i \cap V_i$ is open by (T3). The topology it generates is the **box
items/def-product-topology.md:86:topology** $\mathcal{T}^{\square}$, and $\mathcal{R}$ is a basis for it
items/def-product-topology.md:87:([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]).
items/def-product-topology.md:88:
items/def-product-topology.md:89:**The product topology.** The **product topology** $\mathcal{T}^{\Pi}$ on
items/def-product-topology.md:90:$\prod_i X_i$ is the initial topology of the family of projections
items/def-product-topology.md:91:$(\pi_i)_{i \in I}$ ([[def-initial-and-final-topology]]): the topology generated
items/def-product-topology.md:92:by the subbasis
items/def-product-topology.md:93:
items/def-product-topology.md:94:$$\mathcal{G} \;:=\; \{\, \pi_i^{-1}[U] : i \in I,\ U \in \mathcal{T}_i \,\}, \qquad \pi_i^{-1}[U] = \prod_{j \in I} W_j \ \text{ with } W_i = U \text{ and } W_j = X_j \text{ for } j \ne i .$$
items/def-product-topology.md:95:
items/def-product-topology.md:96:By [[thm-basis-criterion]] the finite intersections of members of $\mathcal{G}$
items/def-product-topology.md:97:form a basis for $\mathcal{T}^{\Pi}$, and those finite intersections are exactly
items/def-product-topology.md:98:the boxes with all but finitely many factors unrestricted:
items/def-product-topology.md:99:
items/def-product-topology.md:100:$$\mathcal{R}^{\Pi} \;=\; \Big\{\, \prod_{i \in I} U_i \;:\; U_i \in \mathcal{T}_i \text{ for every } i, \text{ and } U_i = X_i \text{ for all but finitely many } i \,\Big\}.$$
items/def-product-topology.md:101:
items/def-product-topology.md:102:Indeed the intersection of $\pi_{i_1}^{-1}[U_1], \dots, \pi_{i_n}^{-1}[U_n]$ is
items/def-product-topology.md:103:the box whose factor at $i$ is the intersection of those $U_m$ with $i_m = i$ and
items/def-product-topology.md:104:is $X_i$ when no $i_m$ equals $i$; and the intersection of no members is the
items/def-product-topology.md:105:whole product, the box with every factor $X_i$. Conversely a box with
items/def-product-topology.md:106:$U_i = X_i$ off a finite set is such an intersection. Members of
items/def-product-topology.md:107:$\mathcal{R}^{\Pi}$ are called **basic product-open** sets, and members of
items/def-product-topology.md:108:$\mathcal{R}$ **boxes**. So $\mathcal{R}^{\Pi} \subseteq \mathcal{R}$, with
items/def-product-topology.md:109:equality when $I$ is a natural number.
items/def-product-topology.md:110:
items/def-product-topology.md:111:**The empty product.** For $I = \varnothing$ there is exactly one function with
items/def-product-topology.md:112:domain $\varnothing$, the empty function, so $\prod_{i \in \varnothing} X_i$ is a
items/def-product-topology.md:113:one-point set. A one-point set carries exactly one topology, namely
items/def-product-topology.md:114:$\{\varnothing, \{\varnothing\}\}$, since a topology must contain the empty set
items/def-product-topology.md:115:and the whole set and there is nothing else to contain
items/def-product-topology.md:116:([[def-topological-space]]); so the box topology and the product topology agree
items/def-product-topology.md:117:there, and both equal the discrete topology and the indiscrete topology
items/def-product-topology.md:118:([[def-standard-topologies]]), which coincide on a one-point set. There are no
items/def-product-topology.md:119:projections to speak of, and the initial topology of the empty family is indeed
items/def-product-topology.md:120:the indiscrete one ([[def-initial-and-final-topology]]).
items/def-product-topology.md:121:
items/def-product-topology.md:122:**Convention.** Unless the box topology is named explicitly, $\prod_i X_i$ always
items/def-product-topology.md:123:carries the product topology in this library. That is not a matter of taste: the
items/def-product-topology.md:124:product topology is the one with the characteristic property of the next item,
items/def-product-topology.md:125:and the box topology has no such property.
items/def-product-topology.md:126:
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:35:## Statement
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:36:
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:37:Let $X$ be a set, and for $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ write
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:38:
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:39:$$\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{B}} := \{\, U \subseteq X : \text{for every } x \in U \text{ there is } B \in \mathcal{B} \text{ with } x \in B \subseteq U \,\} .$$
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:40:
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:41:1. $\mathcal{B}$ is a basis for some topology on $X$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]])
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:42:   if and only if
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:43:
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:44:   - **(B1)** $\bigcup \mathcal{B} = X$, and
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:45:   - **(B2)** for all $B_1, B_2 \in \mathcal{B}$ and every $x \in B_1 \cap B_2$
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:46:     there is $B_3 \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B_3 \subseteq B_1 \cap B_2$.
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:47:
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:48:   When (B1) and (B2) hold, that topology is **unique**: it is
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:49:   $\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{B}}$, which is also exactly the family of all unions of
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:50:   subfamilies of $\mathcal{B}$.
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:51:
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:52:2. Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$ be an arbitrary family and let
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:53:   $\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}$ be the family of intersections of finitely many
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:54:   members of $\mathcal{S}$. Then $\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}$ satisfies (B1) and
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:55:   (B2), and $\mathcal{T}_{\mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}} = \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$,
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:56:   the topology generated by $\mathcal{S}$. So the finite intersections of any
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:57:   subbasis form a basis for the topology it generates.
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:58:
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:59:**The nullary intersection: this library takes the empty intersection to be $X$.**
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:60:In claim 2 the phrase "finitely many" includes **none**, and the intersection of
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:61:the empty subfamily of $\mathcal{S}$ is $X$, because the defining condition "lies
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:62:in every member of the empty family" holds of every point of $X$. Hence
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:63:$X \in \mathcal{B}_{\mathcal{S}}$ for every $\mathcal{S}$, including
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:64:$\mathcal{S} = \varnothing$, and **no covering hypothesis is imposed on a
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:65:subbasis**. The competing convention takes only nonempty finite intersections and
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:66:compensates by requiring $\bigcup \mathcal{S} = X$; under it claim 2 holds
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:67:verbatim once that hypothesis is added, and the two conventions differ only in
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:68:which of the two devices supplies (B1). The choice made here is recorded again
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:69:among this page's conventions, and it is the reason
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:70:$\langle \varnothing \rangle$ comes out as the indiscrete topology
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:71:$\{\varnothing, X\}$ rather than being undefined.
items/thm-basis-criterion.md:72:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:34:## Definition
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:35:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:36:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]).
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:37:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:38:A family $\mathcal{B} \subseteq \mathcal{T}$ is a **basis for $\mathcal{T}$** if
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:39:every open set is a union of members of $\mathcal{B}$: for every
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:40:$U \in \mathcal{T}$ there is $\mathcal{B}_U \subseteq \mathcal{B}$ with
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:41:$U = \bigcup \mathcal{B}_U$. Equivalently, and this is the form used in proofs,
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:42:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:43:$$\text{for every } U \in \mathcal{T} \text{ and every } x \in U \text{ there is } B \in \mathcal{B} \text{ with } x \in B \subseteq U .$$
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:44:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:45:*The two forms say the same thing.* If every open $U$ is such a union and
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:46:$x \in U$, then $x$ lies in one of the sets united, which is a member of
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:47:$\mathcal{B}$ inside $U$. Conversely, if the displayed condition holds then
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:48:$U = \bigcup \{\, B \in \mathcal{B} : B \subseteq U \,\}$, since each such $B$ is
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:49:contained in $U$ and each $x \in U$ lies in one of them. Note that
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:50:$\bigcup \varnothing = \varnothing$, so the empty open set is covered by the empty
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:51:subfamily and needs no member of $\mathcal{B}$. The members of a basis are called
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:52:**basic open sets**.
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:53:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:54:**The topology generated by a family.** Let $\mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{P}(X)$
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:55:be *any* family of subsets of $X$. Then
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:56:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:57:$$\langle \mathcal{S} \rangle := \bigcap \{\, \mathcal{T}' : \mathcal{T}' \text{ is a topology on } X \text{ with } \mathcal{S} \subseteq \mathcal{T}' \,\}$$
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:58:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:59:is a topology on $X$, it contains $\mathcal{S}$, and it is contained in every
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:60:topology on $X$ that contains $\mathcal{S}$. It is called the **topology
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:61:generated by $\mathcal{S}$**, and $\mathcal{S}$ is a **subbasis** for a topology
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:62:$\mathcal{T}$ when $\mathcal{T} = \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$.
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:63:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:64:*This is well posed, and the obligation is discharged here.* The collection being
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:65:intersected is nonempty, because $\mathcal{P}(X)$ is a topology on $X$ containing
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:66:$\mathcal{S}$; so the intersection is an intersection of a nonempty family of
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:67:subsets of $\mathcal{P}(X)$ and is a set. It is a topology: $\varnothing$ and $X$
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:68:lie in every topology on $X$, hence in the intersection, which is (T1); if
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:69:$\mathcal{S}' \subseteq \langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$ then $\mathcal{S}'$ is a
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:70:subfamily of each $\mathcal{T}'$ in the collection, so $\bigcup \mathcal{S}'$ lies
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:71:in each $\mathcal{T}'$ and hence in the intersection, which is (T2); and the same
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:72:argument with $U \cap V$ gives (T3). It contains $\mathcal{S}$ because every
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:73:$\mathcal{T}'$ in the collection does, and it is contained in each such
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:74:$\mathcal{T}'$ because an intersection is contained in each of its members. So
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:75:$\langle \mathcal{S} \rangle$ is the coarsest topology on $X$ containing
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:76:$\mathcal{S}$ ([[def-topological-space]]), and in particular it is uniquely
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:77:determined by $\mathcal{S}$.
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:78:
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:79:**Neither notion is intrinsic to the family alone.** "$\mathcal{B}$ is a basis
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:80:for $\mathcal{T}$" and "$\mathcal{S}$ is a subbasis for $\mathcal{T}$" are
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:81:relations between a family and a topology, not properties of the family. The
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:82:question of which families are a basis *for some* topology, and how the topology
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:83:generated by a subbasis is computed from it, is settled by the next item.
items/def-topology-basis-subbasis.md:84:
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:44:## Statement
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:45:
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:46:Let $\mathbb{R}$ be a complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]]).
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:47:Then $\mathbb{R}$ is **uncountable** ([[def-countable]]): there is no surjection
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:48:$\mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$, so $\mathbb{R}$ is neither finite nor countably
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:49:infinite.
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:50:
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:51:The proof is Cantor's original argument of 1874, not the decimal diagonal.
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:52:Assuming a surjection $f : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$, one builds nested closed
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:53:intervals $[a_n, b_n]$ with $a_n < b_n$ and $f(n) \notin [a_{n+1}, b_{n+1}]$, and
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:54:then $\sup\{a_n\}$ is a real number that $f$ misses. **The decimal diagonal is
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:55:deliberately avoided**: decimal expansions are infinite series, which this
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:56:library has not yet constructed, so a diagonal proof here would rest on machinery
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:57:that does not exist. The diagonal argument survives in its non-circular form, on
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:58:power sets, as Cantor's theorem earlier on this page; see the remarks below.
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:59:
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:60:**The construction uses no choice, and that is what the thirds are for.** Given
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:61:$[a_n, b_n]$ of length $d$, its three closed thirds
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:62:$[a_n, a_n + d/3]$, $[a_n + d/3, a_n + 2d/3]$, $[a_n + 2d/3, b_n]$ cannot all
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:63:contain $f(n)$, because the first and the third are disjoint; the rule takes the
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:64:**first one in that fixed order** which does not contain $f(n)$. That is a
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:65:definition by cases, so the whole construction is a single application of the
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:66:recursion theorem ([[thm-recursion]]) to one explicitly given function. A version
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:67:of the argument that says "pick a third avoiding $f(n)$" would be using dependent
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:68:choice, silently and unnecessarily.
items/thm-r-uncountable.md:69:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:42:## Statement
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:43:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:44:Let $A$ be a nonempty set. Then $A$ is at most countable ([[def-countable]]) if
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:45:and only if there is a surjection $s : \mathbb{N} \to A$
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:46:([[def-injection-surjection-bijection]]).
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:47:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:48:Moreover, from any such surjection an injection $A \to \mathbb{N}$ is obtained
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:49:explicitly, without any choice, by
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:50:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:51:$$j(a) := \min\{\, k \in \mathbb{N} : s(k) = a \,\}.$$
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:52:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:53:This is the working form of countability used everywhere below: to prove a
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:54:nonempty set countable it suffices to list its elements, repetitions and all.
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:55:
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:56:**No choice principle is used.** The backward direction is where an appeal to
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:57:choice would be natural ("for each $a$ pick some $k$ with $s(k) = a$") and it is
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:58:avoided outright, because $\min$ is canonical: every nonempty set of naturals has
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:59:a *least* element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]), so $j(a)$ is determined by
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:60:$a$ and $s$ alone.
items/lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n.md:61:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:31:## Definition
items/def-compactness-variants.md:32:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:33:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
items/def-compactness-variants.md:34:open covers, subcovers, finiteness and compactness as in [[def-compact-space]],
items/def-compactness-variants.md:35:and *finite*, *at most countable* and *uncountable* as in [[def-countable]].
items/def-compactness-variants.md:36:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:37:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **countably compact** when every open cover of $X$ that
items/def-compactness-variants.md:38:  is at most countable has a finite subcover.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:39:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **Lindel&ouml;f** when every open cover of $X$ has an at
items/def-compactness-variants.md:40:  most countable subcover.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:41:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **sequentially compact** when every sequence $(x_k)$ in
items/def-compactness-variants.md:42:  $X$, that is every function $x : \mathbb{N} \to X$
items/def-compactness-variants.md:43:  ([[def-sequence-convergence-top]]), has a subsequence $(x_{n_j})$ converging to
items/def-compactness-variants.md:44:  a point of $X$, the index map $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ being strictly
items/def-compactness-variants.md:45:  increasing ([[def-sequence]], [[lem-index-map-grows]]).
items/def-compactness-variants.md:46:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **limit point compact** when every infinite subset
items/def-compactness-variants.md:47:  $A \subseteq X$ has a limit point in $X$, that is a point $p \in X$ every
items/def-compactness-variants.md:48:  neighbourhood $N$ of which satisfies $N \cap (A \setminus \{p\}) \ne \varnothing$
items/def-compactness-variants.md:49:  ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]). Here *infinite* means not finite in
items/def-compactness-variants.md:50:  the sense of [[def-countable]].
items/def-compactness-variants.md:51:- $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is **$\sigma$-compact** when there is an at most countable
items/def-compactness-variants.md:52:  family $\mathcal{K}$ of compact subsets of $X$ with $X = \bigcup \mathcal{K}$.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:53:- A subset $A \subseteq X$ is **relatively compact in $X$** when its closure
items/def-compactness-variants.md:54:  $\overline{A}$ ([[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]) is a compact subset of
items/def-compactness-variants.md:55:  $X$.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:56:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:57:A subset $A \subseteq X$ is called countably compact, Lindel&ouml;f,
items/def-compactness-variants.md:58:sequentially compact, limit point compact or $\sigma$-compact when the subspace
items/def-compactness-variants.md:59:$(A, \mathcal{T}_A)$ is ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]), exactly as for
items/def-compactness-variants.md:60:compactness. **Relative compactness is the exception and is deliberately not of
items/def-compactness-variants.md:61:that form**: it is a statement about $A$ *inside* $X$, since $\overline{A}$ is
items/def-compactness-variants.md:62:computed in $X$, and a set may be relatively compact in one space and not in
items/def-compactness-variants.md:63:another that contains it. Every other notion on this list is intrinsic to the
items/def-compactness-variants.md:64:subspace.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:65:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:66:**The countable covers may be listed.** A nonempty at most countable family
items/def-compactness-variants.md:67:$\mathcal{U}$ admits a surjection $\mathbb{N} \to \mathcal{U}$
items/def-compactness-variants.md:68:([[lem-countable-iff-surjection-from-n]]), so countable compactness says: for
items/def-compactness-variants.md:69:every sequence $(U_n)_{n \in \mathbb{N}}$ of open sets with
items/def-compactness-variants.md:70:$X = \bigcup_{n \in \mathbb{N}} U_n$ there are finitely many indices whose sets
items/def-compactness-variants.md:71:already cover $X$. That surjection is produced from the countability assumption
items/def-compactness-variants.md:72:alone and no choice principle is involved; the empty family covers only the empty
items/def-compactness-variants.md:73:space, which is compact anyway.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:74:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:75:**Indexing starts at $0$.** A sequence here is a function on $\mathbb{N}$ and
items/def-compactness-variants.md:76:$\mathbb{N}$ contains $0$ ([[def-sequence]]), so a subsequence is
items/def-compactness-variants.md:77:$(x_{n_j})_{j \in \mathbb{N}}$ with $n_0 < n_1 < \cdots$ and $n_j \ge j$
items/def-compactness-variants.md:78:([[lem-index-map-grows]]). An index range taken from a text that starts at $1$
items/def-compactness-variants.md:79:must be shifted before it is used here.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:80:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:81:**Agreement with the metric definitions.** Let $(X,d)$ be a metric space carrying
items/def-compactness-variants.md:82:its metric topology $\mathcal{T}_d$ ([[def-metric-topology]],
items/def-compactness-variants.md:83:[[def-metrizable-space]]). Then the three notions that
items/def-compactness-variants.md:84:[[def-metric-compactness-variants]] defines metrically are the three defined
items/def-compactness-variants.md:85:above, read in $(X, \mathcal{T}_d)$:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:86:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:87:- *Countably compact.* The open sets used there are the members of
items/def-compactness-variants.md:88:  $\mathcal{T}_d$, so the at most countable open covers are the same families and
items/def-compactness-variants.md:89:  the condition is the same condition, exactly as for compactness itself
items/def-compactness-variants.md:90:  ([[thm-compactness-agrees-with-metric-compactness]]).
items/def-compactness-variants.md:91:- *Sequentially compact.* Convergence of a sequence in the metric sense and in
items/def-compactness-variants.md:92:  the sense of [[def-sequence-convergence-top]] agree on a metric topology,
items/def-compactness-variants.md:93:  because the balls around a point are a neighbourhood base at it
items/def-compactness-variants.md:94:  ([[def-metrizable-space]]); the subsequences quantified over are the same.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:95:- *Limit point compact.* A point $p$ is a limit point of $A$ in the metric sense
items/def-compactness-variants.md:96:  when every ball around $p$ meets $A \setminus \{p\}$, and in the sense above
items/def-compactness-variants.md:97:  when every neighbourhood does; the same neighbourhood base makes the two
items/def-compactness-variants.md:98:  conditions one ([[def-metrizable-space]],
items/def-compactness-variants.md:99:  [[def-interior-closure-boundary-top]]).
items/def-compactness-variants.md:100:
items/def-compactness-variants.md:101:So no statement below about a metrizable space introduces a second notion, and
items/def-compactness-variants.md:102:every theorem of the metric development about these three properties may be
items/def-compactness-variants.md:103:quoted here once a metric inducing the topology is named. Lindel&ouml;fness,
items/def-compactness-variants.md:104:$\sigma$-compactness and relative compactness have no metric counterpart in this
items/def-compactness-variants.md:105:library and are defined here for the first time.
items/def-compactness-variants.md:106:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:32:## Definition
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:33:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:34:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]) and let
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:35:$S \subseteq X$. The **subspace topology** (also *relative topology*) on $S$ is
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:36:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:37:$$\mathcal{T}_S := \{\, U \cap S : U \in \mathcal{T} \,\},$$
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:38:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:39:the family of **traces** on $S$ of the open sets of $X$. The pair
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:40:$(S, \mathcal{T}_S)$ is a **subspace** of $X$. A subset of $S$ that lies in
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:41:$\mathcal{T}_S$ is said to be **open in $S$**, and *relatively open* where the
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:42:ambient space needs emphasis.
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:43:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:44:**$\mathcal{T}_S$ is a topology, and this is discharged here.** (T1):
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:45:$\varnothing = \varnothing \cap S$ and $S = X \cap S$ are traces. (T2): if
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:46:$\mathcal{S}' \subseteq \mathcal{T}_S$, choose for each member a set of
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:47:$\mathcal{T}$ tracing to it — no choice principle is needed, since
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:48:$U' := \bigcup \{\, U \in \mathcal{T} : U \cap S \subseteq W \,\}$ is a canonical
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:49:such set for $W \in \mathcal{T}_S$, being open by (T2) in $X$ and satisfying
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:50:$U' \cap S = W$ — and then
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:51:$\bigcup_i (U_i \cap S) = (\bigcup_i U_i) \cap S \in \mathcal{T}_S$ by (T2) in
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:52:$X$. (T3): $(U \cap S) \cap (V \cap S) = (U \cap V) \cap S \in \mathcal{T}_S$ by
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:53:(T3) in $X$.
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:54:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:55:**Closed sets of a subspace are the traces of the closed sets.** A set
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:56:$C \subseteq S$ is closed in $S$ if and only if $C = F \cap S$ for some closed
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:57:$F \subseteq X$. Indeed $S \setminus (U \cap S) = (X \setminus U) \cap S$ and
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:58:$S \setminus (F \cap S) = (X \setminus F) \cap S$, so complementation inside $S$
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:59:matches complementation inside $X$ under tracing.
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:60:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:61:**Bases and subbases trace as well.** If $\mathcal{B}$ is a basis for
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:62:$\mathcal{T}$ ([[def-topology-basis-subbasis]]) then
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:63:$\mathcal{B}_S := \{\, B \cap S : B \in \mathcal{B} \,\}$ is a basis for
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:64:$\mathcal{T}_S$: its members are open in $S$, and for $W = U \cap S$ open in $S$
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:65:and $x \in W$ there is $B \in \mathcal{B}$ with $x \in B \subseteq U$, whence
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:66:$x \in B \cap S \subseteq W$. The same computation with a subbasis $\mathcal{S}$
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:67:shows that $\{\, S_0 \cap S : S_0 \in \mathcal{S} \,\}$ is a subbasis for
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:68:$\mathcal{T}_S$, since tracing commutes with finite intersections and with
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:69:unions.
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:70:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:71:**The inclusion is continuous.** The inclusion map $\iota : S \to X$,
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:72:$\iota(s) = s$, satisfies $\iota^{-1}[U] = U \cap S$ for every $U \subseteq X$,
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:73:so preimages of open sets are open and $\iota$ is continuous
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:74:([[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]], clause (b)). Moreover
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:75:$\mathcal{T}_S$ is the **coarsest** topology on $S$ making $\iota$ continuous: any
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:76:topology on $S$ for which $\iota$ is continuous must contain every
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:77:$\iota^{-1}[U] = U \cap S$, hence contain $\mathcal{T}_S$.
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:78:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:79:**Characteristic property of a map into a subspace.** Let $(Z, \mathcal{T}_Z)$ be
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:80:a topological space and let $g : Z \to S$ be a function. Then
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:81:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:82:$$g \text{ is continuous as a map } Z \to (S,\mathcal{T}_S) \iff \iota \circ g \text{ is continuous as a map } Z \to (X,\mathcal{T}) .$$
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:83:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:84:*Proof.* For $U \in \mathcal{T}$ one has
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:85:$(\iota \circ g)^{-1}[U] = g^{-1}[\iota^{-1}[U]] = g^{-1}[U \cap S]$. If $g$ is
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:86:continuous then each $g^{-1}[U \cap S]$ is open, so $\iota \circ g$ is continuous;
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:87:conversely if $\iota \circ g$ is continuous then for any $W = U \cap S$ open in
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:88:$S$ the set $g^{-1}[W] = (\iota \circ g)^{-1}[U]$ is open, so $g$ is continuous.
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:89:Both directions use only clause (b) of [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]].
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:90:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:91:**Restriction of a continuous map.** If $f : X \to Y$ is continuous and
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:92:$S \subseteq X$, then $f|_S : S \to Y$ is continuous, since
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:93:$(f|_S)^{-1}[V] = f^{-1}[V] \cap S$ is open in $S$ for every open $V \subseteq Y$
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:94:([[def-continuous-map-top]], [[thm-continuity-characterisations-top]]).
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:95:
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:96:**When relative and ambient agree.** If $S$ is open in $X$ then a subset of $S$
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:97:is open in $S$ if and only if it is open in $X$: a trace $U \cap S$ is then an
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:98:intersection of two open sets of $X$, and conversely an open subset of $X$
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:99:contained in $S$ is its own trace. The same statement with "closed" throughout
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:100:holds when $S$ is closed in $X$. Both are used in the pasting lemma of the next
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:101:item, and both fail without the hypothesis: $S$ itself is always open and closed
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:102:in $S$, and need be neither in $X$.
items/def-subspace-topology-top.md:103:

