items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:39:## Definition
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:40:
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:41:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]), with
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:42:subsets carrying the subspace topology ([[def-subspace-topology-top]]) and
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:43:connectedness as in [[def-connected-space]]. Let $x \in X$.
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:44:
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:45:- The **connected component of $x$** is
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:46:  $$C(x) \;:=\; \bigcup \{\, A \subseteq X : x \in A \text{ and } A \text{ is connected} \,\} .$$
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:47:  A **component of $X$** is a set of the form $C(x)$ for some $x \in X$.
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:48:- The **quasicomponent of $x$** is
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:49:  $$Q(x) \;:=\; \bigcap \{\, K \subseteq X : x \in K \text{ and } K \text{ is clopen in } X \,\} .$$
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:50:  A **quasicomponent of $X$** is a set of the form $Q(x)$.
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:51:- $X$ is **totally disconnected** when $C(x) = \{x\}$ for every $x \in X$.
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:52:
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:53:**Both are well posed, and the obligations are discharged here.** The family
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:54:united in the definition of $C(x)$ is nonempty, since the singleton $\{x\}$ is
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:55:connected: a singleton admits no separation, a separation requiring two disjoint
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:56:nonempty pieces. Every member of that family contains $x$, so
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:57:[[thm-unions-of-connected-sets]] claim 1 applies and **$C(x)$ is connected**;
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:58:being a union of every connected set through $x$, it contains each of them, so
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:59:$C(x)$ is *the largest connected subset of $X$ containing $x$*. The family
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:60:intersected in the definition of $Q(x)$ is nonempty as well, since $X$ itself is
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:61:clopen ([[def-topological-space]]), so the intersection is a set; it contains
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:62:$x$, every member doing so.
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:63:
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:64:**Both notions are defined by a property of $X$, not of an ambient space.** A
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:65:component of a subspace $S \subseteq X$ means a component of the space $S$, and
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:66:is written $C_S(y)$ when the space needs naming. The same holds for
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:67:quasicomponents.
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:68:
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:69:**Totally disconnected, spelled out.** $X$ is totally disconnected exactly when
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:70:every connected subset of $X$ has at most one point: if some connected $A$ had
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:71:two points $x \ne y$ then $A \subseteq C(x)$ would give $C(x) \ne \{x\}$, and
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:72:conversely if $C(x) \ne \{x\}$ then $C(x)$ is a connected set with at least two
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:73:points. The empty space is totally disconnected, having no point to test.
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:74:
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:75:**A discrete space is totally disconnected.** Let $X$ carry the discrete topology
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:76:([[def-standard-topologies]]) and let $A \subseteq X$ have two distinct points
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:77:$x, y$. Every subset of $A$ is open in $A$, so $(\{x\}, A \setminus \{x\})$ is a
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:78:pair of open, disjoint, nonempty sets covering $A$, that is a separation. Hence
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:79:no connected subset has two points and every component is a singleton. The
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:80:converse fails: total disconnectedness does not force the topology to be
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:81:discrete.
items/def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent.md:82:
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:41:## Statement
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:42:
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:43:Let $X$ be a topological space, let $C(x)$ and $Q(x)$ be the component and the
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:44:quasicomponent of $x \in X$ ([[def-connected-component-and-quasicomponent]]).
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:45:Then:
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:46:
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:47:1. **Containment.** $C(x) \subseteq Q(x)$.
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:48:2. **Closedness.** $Q(x)$ is closed in $X$.
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:49:3. **Saturation.** If $y \in Q(x)$ then $Q(y) = Q(x)$; consequently
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:50:   $C(y) \subseteq Q(x)$ for every $y \in Q(x)$, and
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:51:   $$Q(x) \;=\; \bigcup \{\, C(y) : y \in Q(x) \,\},$$
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:52:   so every quasicomponent is a union of components.
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:53:4. **Partition.** The quasicomponents are nonempty, pairwise disjoint, and cover
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:54:   $X$.
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:55:
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:56:**No converse is asserted.** Claim 1 is an inclusion and this theorem does not
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:57:claim it is an equality; the question of when $C(x) = Q(x)$ is not settled on
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:58:this page, and nothing here may be read as settling it.
items/thm-quasicomponents-contain-components.md:59:
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:41:## Statement
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:42:
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:43:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a Hausdorff topological space
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:44:([[def-hausdorff-space]], [[def-topological-space]]), with compact subsets as in
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:45:[[def-compact-space]]. Then:
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:46:
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:47:1. **A point and a disjoint compact set are separated.** If $K \subseteq X$ is
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:48:   compact and $x \in X \setminus K$, there are $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ with
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:49:   $$x \in U, \qquad K \subseteq V, \qquad U \cap V = \varnothing .$$
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:50:2. **Two disjoint compact sets are separated.** If $K, L \subseteq X$ are compact
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:51:   and $K \cap L = \varnothing$, there are $U, V \in \mathcal{T}$ with
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:52:   $$L \subseteq U, \qquad K \subseteq V, \qquad U \cap V = \varnothing .$$
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:53:3. **Compact implies closed.** Every compact subset of $X$ is closed in $X$.
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:54:4. **In a compact Hausdorff space the two classes coincide.** If in addition
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:55:   $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact, then a subset of $X$ is compact if and only if
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:56:   it is closed.
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:57:
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:58:**The proof is written choice-free, and that is not a stylistic preference.** The
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:59:textbook argument says "for each $y \in K$ choose disjoint open $U_y, V_y$",
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:60:which is a selection over an arbitrary index set and therefore an appeal to the
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:61:full Axiom of Choice. What is done below instead is to take the family of **all**
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:62:open $V$ that admit some open $U \ni x$ disjoint from them — a family cut out by
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:63:a formula, with nothing selected — extract a finite subcover from it, and only
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:64:then make finitely many selections, which [[lem-finite-choice]] supplies as a
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:65:theorem of ZF.
items/thm-compact-subset-of-a-hausdorff-space-is-closed.md:66:
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:41:## Statement
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:42:
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:43:Let $(X, \mathcal{T})$ be a topological space ([[def-topological-space]]). For a
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:44:family $\mathcal{A}$ of subsets of $X$ write
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:45:
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:46:$$\bigcap \mathcal{A} \;:=\; \{\, x \in X : x \in A \text{ for every } A \in \mathcal{A} \,\},$$
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:47:
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:48:so that $\bigcap \varnothing = X$, matching the convention for the empty finite
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:49:intersection in [[def-finite-intersection-property]]. Then:
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:50:
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:51:1. $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact ([[def-compact-space]]) **if and only if** every
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:52:   family $\mathcal{A}$ of closed subsets of $X$ with the finite intersection
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:53:   property ([[def-finite-intersection-property]]) satisfies
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:54:   $\bigcap \mathcal{A} \ne \varnothing$.
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:55:2. Equivalently: $(X, \mathcal{T})$ is compact if and only if every family of
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:56:   closed subsets of $X$ that is contained in some filter on $X$
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:57:   ([[def-filter]]) has nonempty intersection, a family of subsets of $X$ lying
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:58:   in a filter exactly when it has the finite intersection property
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:59:   ([[lem-fip-generates-filter]]).
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:60:
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:61:No choice principle is used in either direction: complementation is a canonical
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:62:bijection, so no member of a family ever has to be selected.
items/thm-compact-iff-fip.md:63:

