research/plan-spec.json:20315:          "strategy": "direct, in two clauses, both by reduction to the published one-dimensional theory rather than by re-running its proofs. Clause 1: absolute convergence gives a Cauchy sequence of partial sums, by the triangle inequality for $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]]) and the Cauchy criterion for the real series [[thm-series-cauchy-criterion]]; then completeness ([[thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness]]) gives convergence. Clause 2: each coordinate series converges absolutely because $\\lvert (x_k)_i\\rvert \\le \\lVert x_k\\rVert_2$ and [[thm-direct-comparison-test]], so [[thm-dirichlet-rearrangement]] applies coordinatewise, and [[thm-componentwise-convergence-and-completeness]] reassembles the sum. State the consequence in the form the next items use: if $\\sum x_k$ converges absolutely then $\\mathcal{S}(x)$ is the single point $\\{\\sum x_k\\}$. Note that [[lem-absolute-convergence-implies-convergence]] is the published one-dimensional statement and that this theorem is its $\\mathbb{R}^n$ analogue, NOT a generalisation of its proof.",
research/plan-spec.json:20335:          "strategy": "definition, PHRASED WITH THE INNER PRODUCT AND NOT WITH LINEAR FUNCTIONALS, and that is a deliberate design decision forced by plan order: the page that introduces linear maps and dual spaces sits earlier in plan order but is not built, so no linear-map vocabulary is available here. For a convergent series $\\sum x_k$ in $\\mathbb{R}^n$ set $\\Gamma := \\{\\,a \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : \\sum_k \\lvert\\langle a, x_k\\rangle\\rvert \\text{ converges}\\,\\}$ and $\\Gamma^{\\perp} := \\{\\,y \\in \\mathbb{R}^n : \\langle a,y\\rangle = 0 \\text{ for every } a \\in \\Gamma\\,\\}$. PROVE BOTH ARE LINEAR SUBSPACES ([[def-linear-subspace]], [[lem-linear-subspace-criterion]]): for $\\Gamma$ use bilinearity and [[lem-series-linearity]] with [[thm-direct-comparison-test]]; for $\\Gamma^{\\perp}$ use bilinearity alone. Record $0 \\in \\Gamma$ always, and $\\Gamma = \\mathbb{R}^n$ exactly when the series converges absolutely (one direction by comparison with $\\lVert x_k\\rVert_2$ using [[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]], the other by testing against the standard basis and [[lem-every-norm-on-rn-is-continuous-for-the-euclidean-metric]]'s comparison chain) -- that equivalence is what makes the next theorem contain [[thm-absolute-convergence-in-rn]] as a special case. Define AFFINE SUBSPACE as a coset $s + W$ of a linear subspace, since the library has no such notion yet, and prove the coset is determined by $W$ and any of its points.",
research/plan-spec.json:20486:          "strategy": "counterexample with a two-line computation and a precise conclusion. Take $x = e_0$, $y = e_1$: $\\lVert x+y\\rVert_1^{2}+\\lVert x-y\\rVert_1^{2} = 4+4 = 8$ while $2\\lVert x\\rVert_1^{2}+2\\lVert y\\rVert_1^{2} = 4$. Since every norm arising from an inner product satisfies the parallelogram law ([[thm-cauchy-schwarz-and-the-euclidean-norm]] clause (b)), no inner product on $\\mathbb{R}^{2}$ induces $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_1$. STATE THE LOGIC PRECISELY: the page proves the parallelogram law only for the Euclidean inner product, so the conclusion available here is about norms of that form; a general converse (the Jordan-von Neumann theorem, that the parallelogram law characterises inner-product norms) is not proved on this page and must not be asserted. Note also that $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_1$ IS equivalent to $\\lVert\\cdot\\rVert_2$ by [[thm-all-norms-on-rn-are-equivalent]] -- equivalence of norms is a metric statement and says nothing about inner products, which is the point worth making.",
