items/def-sequence.md:47:## Definition
items/def-sequence.md:48:
items/def-sequence.md:49:Throughout, $\mathbb{R}$ is the complete ordered field ([[def-complete-ordered-field]],
items/def-sequence.md:50:[[def-real-numbers]]) with its order and absolute value ([[def-real-order]]), and
items/def-sequence.md:51:$\mathbb{N}$ is the set of natural numbers with its order ([[def-natural-numbers]],
items/def-sequence.md:52:[[def-nat-order]]).
items/def-sequence.md:53:
items/def-sequence.md:54:A **sequence of reals** is a function $x : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{R}$. We write
items/def-sequence.md:55:$x_k$ for the value $x(k)$, call it the **$k$-th term**, and write $(x_k)$, or
items/def-sequence.md:56:$(x_k)_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$, for the function itself. The **range** of $(x_k)$ is
items/def-sequence.md:57:the subset $\{\, x_k : k \in \mathbb{N} \,\} \subseteq \mathbb{R}$.
items/def-sequence.md:58:
items/def-sequence.md:59:Let $(x_k)$ be a sequence of reals and let $P$ be a property of indices.
items/def-sequence.md:60:
items/def-sequence.md:61:- $(x_k)$ is **bounded** if there is $M \in \mathbb{R}$ with $|x_k| \le M$ for
items/def-sequence.md:62:  every $k \in \mathbb{N}$; it is **unbounded** otherwise.
items/def-sequence.md:63:- $P$ holds **eventually** if there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$ such that $P(k)$ holds
items/def-sequence.md:64:  for every $k \ge K$.
items/def-sequence.md:65:- $P$ holds **frequently** if for every $K \in \mathbb{N}$ there is some
items/def-sequence.md:66:  $k \ge K$ for which $P(k)$ holds.
items/def-sequence.md:67:- For $K \in \mathbb{N}$, the **$K$-th tail** of $(x_k)$ is the sequence
items/def-sequence.md:68:  $x^{(K)}$ defined by $x^{(K)}_j := x_{j + K}$ for $j \in \mathbb{N}$; it is
items/def-sequence.md:69:  again a sequence of reals.
items/def-sequence.md:70:- A function $n : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$ is **strictly increasing** if
items/def-sequence.md:71:  $n_j < n_k$ whenever $j < k$. For such an $n$, the **subsequence** of $(x_k)$
items/def-sequence.md:72:  along $n$ is the composite $x \circ n$, written $(x_{n_k})_{k \in \mathbb{N}}$;
items/def-sequence.md:73:  it is again a sequence of reals.
items/def-sequence.md:74:
items/def-sequence.md:75:**Convergence and Cauchyness are not defined here.** They are already fixed, for
items/def-sequence.md:76:sequences of reals, by the published [[def-real-limit]]: $(x_k)$ *converges* to
items/def-sequence.md:77:$x \in \mathbb{R}$ when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is
items/def-sequence.md:78:$K \in \mathbb{N}$ with $|x_k - x| < \varepsilon$ for all $k \ge K$, and $(x_k)$
items/def-sequence.md:79:is *Cauchy* when for every rational $\varepsilon > 0$ there is $K \in \mathbb{N}$
items/def-sequence.md:80:with $|x_k - x_l| < \varepsilon$ for all $k, l \ge K$. This page builds the
items/def-sequence.md:81:toolkit for those two notions and does not restate them. A sequence
items/def-sequence.md:82:**converges** if it converges to some real, and **diverges** if it does not.
